QB.push(
{id:1681,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"Anthropic's \"Building effective agents\" taxonomy places orchestrator-workers among the workflows, even though the orchestrator decides at runtime which subtasks exist. What makes that classification correct?",
o:["A. The orchestrator's delegate-and-synthesise control path is itself predefined code; only the subtask list varies","B. Workers are LLM calls rather than autonomous agents, and any system built only from LLM calls is a workflow","C. The orchestrator is permitted a fixed maximum number of workers, and bounded iteration is what defines a workflow","D. Orchestrator-workers always runs its workers in parallel, and parallel systems are classified as workflows"],a:0,
e:"Anthropic separates the two by **who owns control flow**. Workflows are systems where \"LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths\"; agents are systems where the LLM \"dynamically direct[s] their own processes and tool usage.\" In orchestrator-workers the shape of the run — decompose, fan out, synthesise — is written by you in code. What varies is the *content* of the subtask list, not the path. Runtime data-dependence is not self-direction. The transferable test: ask whether the LLM chooses the next structural step, or merely fills a slot your code already defined.",
w:"B — worker identity is irrelevant; agents are also just LLM calls, and the distinction is control flow. C — bounded iteration is a useful guardrail but is not the taxonomy's criterion; agents are usually bounded too. D — parallelism is orthogonal; prompt chaining is sequential and still a workflow, and agents can call tools in parallel."},

{id:1682,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A fraud team needs a high-confidence yes/no on whether a transaction narrative describes money laundering. They send the same narrative to the same model three times with three differently worded prompts and accept the majority verdict. In Anthropic's taxonomy, what have they built?",
o:["A. Evaluator-optimiser, because multiple assessments refine a single judgement","B. Parallelisation, voting variant","C. Orchestrator-workers, because one decision is decomposed into three worker calls","D. Routing, because each prompt directs the input to a different specialised assessment"],a:1,
e:"**Voting** is one of the two parallelisation variants: \"running the same task multiple times to get diverse outputs.\" The subtask is identical, the calls are independent and concurrent, and aggregation happens in your code — here a majority rule. Voting is the right reach when you want higher confidence on one judgement and will pay N× the tokens for it. Contrast **sectioning**, the other variant, where each parallel call handles a *different* part of the work. Neither involves feedback between calls, which is what separates parallelisation from evaluator-optimiser.",
w:"A — evaluator-optimiser is a sequential feedback loop where a critique drives a revision; these three calls never see each other. C — nothing is decomposed; all three calls do the whole task, and no orchestrator LLM plans a split. D — routing sends one input to *one* selected destination; here every call receives the same input."},

{id:1683,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"The same fraud team finds their three-way vote is unanimous on 94% of cases, and that on the 6% where it splits, human reviewers agree with the majority only about half the time. What does this most likely indicate about the design?",
o:["A. The majority threshold is too low; requiring unanimity would raise agreement with reviewers","B. Three samples is too few for a stable majority; nine would converge on the reviewers' answer","C. The three calls share the model's blind spots, so voting is measuring prompt sensitivity rather than genuine confidence","D. The narratives themselves are ambiguous, so no configuration of the pattern can improve the result"],a:2,
e:"Voting only adds information when the errors of the individual attempts are **substantially independent**. Three prompts against one model share the same weights, training distribution and failure modes, so a unanimous vote mostly certifies that the wording did not matter — not that the answer is right. The split cases carry the real signal: they are where phrasing flipped the outcome. Practical fixes are to decorrelate (a different model tier, genuinely different evidence, a rubric-driven evaluator) or to treat splits as a routing signal to a human rather than a vote to resolve.",
w:"A — unanimity changes the aggregation rule, not the correlation between attempts; it buys fewer flagged cases, not better ones. B — more correlated samples converge faster on the *model's* answer, which is the thing in doubt. D — the split subset is exactly where redesign helps; escalating those to a reviewer is a concrete improvement."},

{id:1684,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A code-review service runs four checks on every pull request — security, performance, style and test coverage — as four concurrent model calls, then merges the findings into one report. The four checks are hard-coded and never change. The team's design doc calls this \"orchestrator-workers\". Why is that label wrong, and what is it?",
o:["A. It is routing, because each check is a specialised handler selected for the input","B. It is an agent, because the four calls run without waiting for each other","C. It is evaluator-optimiser, because reviewing a diff is a critique task","D. It is parallelisation by sectioning, because the subtask boundaries are fixed in code rather than decided at runtime"],a:3,
e:"**Sectioning** splits work into independent subtasks whose boundaries you defined in advance; **orchestrator-workers** exists precisely for tasks \"where you can't predict the subtasks needed,\" so an LLM decomposes the job at runtime. Here nothing is decomposed — the four lanes are a constant in the source. The label matters operationally: sectioning has a fixed fan-out you can capacity-plan and test lane by lane, whereas orchestrator-workers has a variable fan-out needing a spawn budget and a synthesis step robust to a worker set you did not anticipate.",
w:"A — routing selects *one* destination per input; all four checks run on every pull request. B — concurrency is not autonomy; the model never chooses what to do next, and the taxonomy classifies self-directed control flow, not parallelism, as the agent property. C — nothing generates then revises against feedback; the checks produce findings once, with no optimiser loop."},

{id:1685,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A support platform routes each inbound ticket to one of six specialised handlers by category. A new requirement lands: some enterprise tickets need work from an unpredictable combination of handlers — sometimes billing plus provisioning, sometimes provisioning plus security plus legal — and which combination applies is only clear after reading the ticket and the customer's account. What is the appropriate architectural change?",
o:["A. Replace the classifier with an orchestrator that plans the required subtasks per ticket, delegates to the handlers as workers, and synthesises their outputs","B. Keep routing and add a seventh \"complex\" route that runs all six handlers and concatenates the results","C. Convert each handler into an autonomous agent and let the ticket be passed between them until one declares it resolved","D. Keep routing but run the classifier repeatedly on the ticket until it stops naming new categories"],a:0,
e:"Routing's defining assumption is a **fixed set of known destinations, one selected per input**. The moment the required *set* of subtasks varies with the input and cannot be enumerated in advance, you have crossed into **orchestrator-workers**: a central LLM \"dynamically breaks down tasks, delegates them to worker LLMs, and synthesizes their results.\" The handlers survive as workers; a planning step replaces the classifier and a synthesis step replaces the single handler's output. Note this is still a workflow — the decompose/delegate/synthesise path is your code.",
w:"B — running all six on every complex ticket pays for irrelevant work and produces a report nobody asked for, with no synthesis step to reconcile overlaps. C — peer-to-peer hand-off gives up central accountability and has no termination guarantee; the requirement is unpredictable *composition*, not unbounded autonomy. D — repeated classification is an ad-hoc planner with no state, no dependency handling and no synthesis, and it can loop."},

{id:1686,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Two evaluator-optimiser loops are proposed for a service that generates SQL migrations. Loop 1 asks a second model call to rate the migration against a written rubric. Loop 2 applies the migration to a scratch database and feeds back the actual error output and row counts. Both cost about the same. Which is the stronger design, and why?",
o:["A. Loop 1, because a rubric can assess intent and readability, which a database cannot","B. Loop 2, because the feedback is an objective, externally generated signal the generator cannot talk its way past","C. Neither, because evaluator-optimiser requires the evaluator and generator to be different model tiers","D. Loop 1, because model-generated critiques can be iterated in-context without external infrastructure"],a:1,
e:"Evaluator-optimiser is \"particularly effective when we have clear evaluation criteria.\" The sharpest version of clear criteria is a **programmatic oracle** — a test suite, a compiler, a schema validator, a scratch-database apply. Its verdict is independent of the generator's rhetoric and cannot be flattered into passing, so the loop converges on something that *works* rather than something that *reads well*. A model judge shares the generator's blind spots and tends to accept fluent output. Prefer feedback produced by the world over feedback produced by a model, reserving the judge for qualities no oracle can measure.",
w:"A — readability is real but secondary, and nothing stops a rubric pass on top of a passing migration; one that reads well and fails to apply is worthless. C — the pattern has no such requirement; generator and evaluator are frequently the same model, which is a weakness to mitigate rather than a rule. D — avoiding infrastructure is a convenience argument, and the missing infrastructure is exactly what supplies the trustworthy signal."},

{id:1687,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team runs an evaluator-optimiser loop for marketing copy. The generator and the evaluator are the same model, given the same brand-guidelines context, and the evaluator is told to critique the draft. After three rounds the evaluator reliably says the copy is excellent, yet human editors reject about a third of the outputs for the same recurring problem. What is the mechanism behind this?",
o:["A. Three rounds is too few for the loop to converge, so the evaluator has not yet detected the problem","B. The evaluator is reading the draft rather than the brief, so it cannot assess instruction compliance","C. Generator and evaluator share weights and context, so the recurring flaw is invisible to both — the loop is self-consistent, not correct","D. The loop needs a lower effort setting on the evaluator so that it critiques more aggressively"],a:2,
e:"A critique is only useful where the critic's judgement is **independent of the author's**. Same model, same context, same guidelines means the same priors about what good copy looks like, so a systematic blind spot is reproduced on both sides and the iterations converge on mutual agreement. The observable symptom is exactly this: rising internal scores with flat human acceptance. Fixes are to decorrelate the evaluator — a different model, a checklist derived from the *rejections* rather than the guidelines, retrieval of approved exemplars — and to track human acceptance as the objective, never the evaluator's own score.",
w:"A — the internal score is already saturated; more rounds of a blind evaluator produce more agreement, not more detection. B — the evaluator can be given both draft and brief and the problem persists; the failure is shared judgement, not missing input. D — effort or tone settings change how much the evaluator says, not what it is capable of noticing."},

{id:1688,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A three-step prompt chain extracts clauses from a contract, classifies each clause's risk, and drafts a client memo. In production the memo is occasionally confident and entirely wrong because step 1 returned an empty clause list for scanned documents. What is the correct structural fix?",
o:["A. Merge the three steps into one call so the model sees the whole document and cannot lose the clauses","B. Increase the memo step's context so it can re-read the contract and recover the missing clauses","C. Convert the chain into an autonomous agent so it can decide to retry extraction when results look thin","D. Add a programmatic gate after step 1 that validates the extraction and halts or reroutes the chain before step 2 runs"],a:3,
e:"Prompt chaining's advantage over one big call is precisely that it \"allows you to add programmatic checks on any intermediate steps.\" A **gate** is deterministic code between stages asserting the invariants the next stage depends on — clause count greater than zero, page count reconciled, no OCR-failure marker — and on failure it stops the chain or routes to a scanned-document path. Without gates a chain launders a step-1 failure into a step-3 assertion, which is worse than an error because it looks like an answer. Every chain boundary is a free place to fail loudly.",
w:"A — a single call removes the inspection point that would have caught this and makes the failure harder to localise, while the unreadable-scan cause remains. B — re-reading duplicates step 1's work inside a step not designed for it, and the memo step cannot know extraction was incomplete without a check. C — autonomy is an expensive substitute for one boolean assertion, and the model's judgement of \"looks thin\" is less reliable than a count."},

{id:1689,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"According to Anthropic's guidance, what does prompt chaining trade away, and in exchange for what?",
o:["A. It accepts higher latency in exchange for higher accuracy on each subtask","B. It accepts higher token cost in exchange for lower latency through parallel execution","C. It accepts reduced accuracy in exchange for a simpler codebase","D. It accepts loss of auditability in exchange for fewer model calls"],a:0,
e:"Anthropic frames chaining as \"trading off latency for higher accuracy by making each LLM call an easier task.\" The steps are **sequential**, so wall-clock time is the sum of the steps plus your gate logic — strictly worse than one call. What you buy is that each call has a narrower job, a smaller instruction set and less to get wrong, plus inspection points between stages. The trade is worth taking when the task \"can be easily and cleanly decomposed into fixed subtasks\" and accuracy outranks response time; it is a bad trade behind an interactive latency budget.",
w:"B — chaining is sequential by definition; parallelisation is the pattern that buys latency, and only on independent subtasks. C — accuracy is what chaining is *for*, and the code gets more complex, not simpler, because of the gates. D — auditability improves under chaining, since each intermediate output is a discrete artefact you can log and assert on."},

{id:1690,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A bank will let an agent draft and file regulatory breach reports, but compliance requires that for any filed report they can reconstruct, months later, exactly which evidence supported each statement and which decisions a human made. The agent's autonomy is genuinely useful because the investigation path varies. What design satisfies both?",
o:["A. Replace the agent with a fixed workflow, since reconstructability is incompatible with model-directed control flow","B. Keep the agent for the investigation, but persist a structured trajectory — every tool call, its arguments, its result and a provenance-bearing evidence record — and put the filing action behind a deterministic, logged human approval","C. Keep the agent and store the final report plus the model ID and prompt, which together make the run reproducible","D. Keep the agent and lower its effort setting so its reasoning is short enough for a reviewer to read in full"],a:1,
e:"Auditability is a **logging and authorisation** property, not an architectural prohibition on autonomy. A self-directed investigation can be fully reconstructable if you record the trajectory as first-class data — ordered tool calls with arguments and results, evidence items carrying source identifiers, and the human's approve/reject decision with identity and timestamp — and if the irreversible step is executed by code only after that approval. The model proposes, deterministic code authorises and records. Re-running a prompt is not a reconstruction: serving infrastructure changes and output is not deterministic even with a pinned model.",
w:"A — determinism and auditability are different requirements; regulators ask what happened and who approved it, not that the run replays identically. C — a prompt and a model ID do not reproduce a run, and they capture none of the intermediate evidence or human decisions the requirement names. D — shorter reasoning is not a record; it preserves neither evidence provenance nor who authorised the filing."},

{id:1691,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An insurance claims pipeline has three phases: validate the submitted documents against a known checklist, investigate anything that fails validation (an open-ended task requiring varied external lookups), and issue the payment. A team proposes making the whole pipeline one autonomous agent for uniformity. What is the better design and the reason?",
o:["A. One agent throughout, because a single control loop is simpler to observe and reason about than a mixture","B. One agent throughout, but with the payment tool's arguments validated by a JSON schema","C. A hybrid: deterministic code for validation and payment, with an agent scoped to the investigation phase only","D. Three separate agents chained together, each with its own system prompt and tool set"],a:2,
e:"Spend autonomy only where the path is genuinely unknowable. Validation against a known checklist is enumerable, so code does it faster, cheaper, identically and testably. Payment is irreversible and rule-bound, so it belongs in code behind an authorisation check. Investigation is the one phase whose steps depend on what is found, which is Anthropic's criterion for an agent: \"open-ended problems where it's difficult or impossible to predict the required number of steps.\" The result is a workflow with an agentic phase — determinism where it is available, flexibility paid for only where you must.",
w:"A — uniformity buys nothing and costs a great deal: it converts two deterministic, testable phases into non-deterministic ones and puts an irreversible payment inside model-directed control flow. B — schema validation constrains the *shape* of the payment call, not whether it should happen; the model still decides to pay. D — three agents triples the autonomy rather than reducing it and adds hand-off surface between phases that were never in doubt."},

{id:1692,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A team writes its services in Go and wants to use the Claude Agent SDK's agent loop, tools and permission model. What is the accurate position?",
o:["A. The Agent SDK ships in the same seven languages as the client SDKs, so a Go library is available","B. The Agent SDK is TypeScript-only; Go callers must reimplement the loop against the Messages API","C. The Agent SDK has no language bindings and is only usable through the Claude Code terminal interface","D. The Agent SDK is a library for Python and TypeScript only; from other languages you run the CLI as a subprocess in headless mode and parse its JSON output"],a:3,
e:"The **Agent SDK** and the **client SDKs** are different products with different language coverage. The client SDKs for the Messages API span seven languages; the Agent SDK — the same agent loop, built-in tools, hooks, subagents, permissions and sessions that power Claude Code, as a library — is published for **Python and TypeScript**. The documented escape hatch for other languages is to invoke the CLI as a subprocess in headless mode (`-p` with `--output-format json`) and consume structured output. **Currency note:** language coverage moves; check the Agent SDK overview rather than relying on recall.",
w:"A — that count belongs to the client SDKs for the API, not to the Agent SDK. B — Python is supported too, and reimplementing the loop is unnecessary when the CLI can be driven headlessly. C — the SDK is explicitly a library for use in your own process; the CLI is a separate, additional way in."},

{id:1693,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Three teams describe their needs. Team 1 wants a long-running agent that processes overnight jobs with no infrastructure of its own to manage. Team 2 wants full control of the tool loop and to send raw requests to `/v1/messages`. Team 3 wants Claude Code's loop, file tools and permission prompts inside their own Python service. Which mapping is correct?",
o:["A. Team 1 to Managed Agents; Team 2 to the client SDK; Team 3 to the Agent SDK","B. Team 1 to the Agent SDK; Team 2 to Managed Agents; Team 3 to the client SDK","C. Team 1 to the client SDK; Team 2 to the Agent SDK; Team 3 to Managed Agents","D. All three to the Agent SDK, which covers hosted execution, raw API access and in-process embedding"],a:0,
e:"The products separate by **who runs the loop and who runs the sandbox**. **Managed Agents** is a hosted product where Anthropic runs the agent and its sandbox — the fit for long-running asynchronous work you do not want to host. The **client SDK** gives direct access to the Messages API and you implement the tool loop yourself — the fit when you want full control. The **Agent SDK** is a library running Claude Code's agent loop, built-in tools and permission model inside *your* process. Choosing wrongly usually shows up as rebuilding one product's features on top of another.",
w:"B — the Agent SDK runs in your process, so Team 1 would still be managing sandboxes and long-run infrastructure, and Managed Agents is hosted, which is not what Team 2 asked for. C — the client SDK supplies no agent loop for Team 1, and Team 3 asked to embed the loop rather than have it hosted. D — the Agent SDK is neither a hosted service nor a raw API client; it is one option among three."},

{id:1694,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does Anthropic's \"Building effective agents\" recommend as the starting point for developers building agentic systems?",
o:["A. Adopt a graph-based orchestration framework first, since agentic control flow is hard to write by hand","B. Start by using the LLM APIs directly, since many patterns can be implemented in a few lines of code","C. Start with an autonomous agent and remove autonomy only if costs become unacceptable","D. Start with a multi-agent orchestrator, since single-agent designs rarely survive contact with real tasks"],a:1,
e:"The guidance is explicit: \"we suggest that developers start by using LLM APIs directly: many patterns can be implemented in a few lines of code.\" Frameworks are acknowledged as easy on-ramps, but they \"often create extra layers of abstraction that can obscure the underlying prompts and responses, making them harder to debug\" and tempt you into unnecessary complexity. The companion rule is to \"find the simplest solution possible, and only increase complexity when needed\" — and if you do adopt a framework, to understand the code underneath, since \"incorrect assumptions about what's under the hood are a common source of customer error.\"",
w:"A — this inverts the advice; frameworks are the later step, adopted knowingly. C — the recommendation runs the other way: begin with the simplest thing that works and add autonomy only where a predefined path cannot serve. D — multi-agent designs are the most expensive and least predictable starting point, reserved for tasks that need them."},

{id:1695,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which observation most legitimately argues that a team should stop using its agent framework and move to direct API calls?",
o:["A. The framework's release cadence is slower than the team's deployment cadence","B. The framework's configuration is expressed in YAML rather than in the team's primary language","C. Debugging a production incident repeatedly requires reading the framework's source to discover what prompt was actually sent","D. The framework does not expose the newest model identifiers on the day they ship"],a:2,
e:"The cost Anthropic names for frameworks is that abstraction \"can obscure the underlying prompts and responses, making them harder to debug.\" When incident response routinely means reverse-engineering the library to recover the actual request, the abstraction has stopped paying rent: you carry its learning and debugging cost while still needing ground truth. That is the honest exit signal. The general test is whether the framework's persistence, state model, tracing and retries are things you would otherwise build — if yes, keep it; if you are only using it to assemble a prompt, drop it.",
w:"A — release cadence is a dependency-management annoyance, and pinning a version handles it. B — configuration syntax is a taste and tooling concern; it neither obscures the prompt nor duplicates work you would otherwise do. D — model identifiers are usually strings you pass through, and lagging support is a temporary packaging issue rather than a structural problem."},

{id:1696,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A nightly document pipeline takes 40 minutes across 12 stages. When a third-party API fails at stage 9, the whole run restarts from stage 1. A separate requirement has appeared: a reviewer must be able to inspect and edit the intermediate state at stage 6 before the run continues. Which capability of a graph/state-machine framework such as LangGraph addresses both?",
o:["A. Typed tool signatures, which validate each stage's inputs and outputs","B. Automatic retry with exponential backoff around each node","C. Parallel node execution, which shortens the run so restarts cost less","D. Checkpointed graph state persisted per run, which supports resuming from the last successful step and pausing for human input"],a:3,
e:"The substantive thing a graph framework adds over a hand-written loop is an **explicit state object plus a persistence layer**: state is snapshotted after each node, keyed by a run identifier, in a durable store. That one mechanism gives resumability after a crash or outage, human-in-the-loop interrupts (pause at a node, let a person edit state, resume), and a state history you can inspect or replay when debugging. The cost is the abstraction itself — a state schema and a graph API to learn and debug through — so adopt it when durability and resumability are genuine requirements, as they are here.",
w:"A — typing catches shape errors; it neither preserves progress across a process restart nor provides a pause point. B — backoff helps a transient blip but not a sustained outage, and it offers the reviewer no place to intervene. C — a faster run still restarts from zero, and shortening it does nothing for the human-inspection requirement."},

{id:1697,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What do typed agent frameworks such as PydanticAI or Strands principally contribute over an untyped hand-written loop?",
o:["A. Schema-declared tool signatures and validated, typed structured results, so malformed model output fails at a defined boundary rather than downstream","B. Guaranteed determinism, since typed inputs make the model's outputs reproducible","C. Elimination of prompt engineering, because the type definitions replace instructions","D. Lower token cost, because typed schemas are transmitted more compactly than JSON Schema"],a:0,
e:"Typed frameworks push **contract enforcement to the edges of the loop**. Tool parameters and result objects are declared as types, the framework generates the schema the model sees, and it validates and parses what comes back — so a missing field or wrong enum raises at a known boundary with a usable error instead of surfacing three functions later as a null. Combined with native structured output (`output_config.format` with a `json_schema`, or `strict: true` on a tool) this makes model output safe to hand to ordinary code. Typing does not buy determinism, content correctness, or freedom from prompting.",
w:"B — types constrain shape, not sampling; the same typed call can return different valid values, and output is not fully deterministic. C — descriptions and instructions still carry the semantics of when and why to call a tool, which types cannot express. D — the schema still travels as JSON Schema in the tool definition and is billed as input tokens like any other."},

{id:1698,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Anthropic's framework guidance names one specific cost of agent frameworks. What is it?",
o:["A. They lock the application to one model provider, preventing migration","B. They add layers of abstraction that obscure the underlying prompts and responses, making systems harder to debug","C. They prevent the use of tools, since tool schemas must be declared by the framework","D. They forbid parallel tool execution, which caps throughput"],a:1,
e:"The stated concern is abstraction opacity: frameworks \"often create extra layers of abstraction that can obscure the underlying prompts and responses, making them harder to debug. They can also make it tempting to add complexity when a simpler setup would suffice.\" The mitigation given is not avoidance but literacy — if you adopt one, \"ensure you understand the underlying code,\" since \"incorrect assumptions about what's under the hood are a common source of customer error.\" This is a debuggability argument, and it is why teams keep a way to dump the exact request a framework produced.",
w:"A — provider lock-in is a commercial concern some frameworks actually reduce; it is not the cost Anthropic names. C — tool use is a core feature of every such framework, not something they prevent. D — parallel tool execution is widely supported, and where limited that is an API-level setting rather than a framework prohibition."},

{id:1699,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team must pick between two framework styles for a compliance-reporting system whose steps are fully known in advance but which must survive process restarts and produce a per-step audit trail. Style 1 asks you to declare a graph of nodes and edges and runs the model inside it. Style 2 asks you to declare a model, a prompt and a set of tools and lets the model drive the loop. Which fits, and what is the underlying reason?",
o:["A. Style 2, because a model-driven loop adapts to compliance rule changes without code edits","B. Style 1, because a graph framework is always the safer default regardless of the task","C. Style 1, because the control flow is knowable, and a declared graph makes the path, its state and its audit points explicit","D. Style 2, because fewer lines of setup code means fewer places for an audit finding to arise"],a:2,
e:"Match the framework's shape to where control lives. **Workflow-driven** (graph/state-machine) frameworks ask you to write the path and let the model act inside each node — right when the path is knowable, because the graph *is* the specification, the state object is the audit record, and node boundaries are natural persistence and approval points. **Model-driven** frameworks minimise setup by letting the model choose the next step, which is the right trade only when you cannot enumerate the steps. Here the steps are known and the requirements are durability and traceability, which the explicit-path style supplies directly.",
w:"A — adapting without code edits is the opposite of what a compliance system wants; rule changes should be reviewed changes, and model-chosen paths are harder to attest. B — there is no always; a graph is overhead when the path genuinely cannot be predicted, which is when a model-driven loop earns its keep. D — brevity of setup is not an audit property, and the missing lines are exactly the ones that would have recorded the path."},

{id:1700,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"In Anthropic's taxonomy, the basic building block of every agentic system is the augmented LLM. What augments it?",
o:["A. Fine-tuning, quantisation and distillation","B. Streaming, batching and caching","C. A system prompt, a temperature setting and a stop sequence","D. Retrieval, tools and memory"],a:3,
e:"The building block is \"an LLM enhanced with augmentations such as **retrieval, tools, and memory**.\" The important nuance is that these are not passive attachments: models \"can actively use these capabilities — generating their own search queries, selecting appropriate tools, and determining what information to retain.\" Every workflow and agent pattern above it is a way of arranging calls to this block, so the practical advice is to invest first in the block itself — tailoring the augmentations to your use case and making them well documented and easy to call — before reaching for orchestration.",
w:"A — those are model-training and serving techniques, not the augmentations in the taxonomy. B — those are API delivery and cost features; they change how a call is transported and billed, not what the model can reach. C — those are sampling and instruction parameters, two of which are deprecated on current models; none constitutes an augmentation."},

{id:1701,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement best captures how Anthropic characterises the augmented LLM's relationship to its augmentations?",
o:["A. The model actively uses them — generating its own search queries, selecting tools and deciding what to retain","B. The augmentations are applied by surrounding code, which decides what to retrieve and which tools to invoke","C. The augmentations are training-time additions baked into the model's weights","D. The augmentations are only available inside a multi-agent architecture"],a:0,
e:"The point of the building block is **model-driven use of capabilities**: \"models can actively use these capabilities — generating their own search queries, selecting appropriate tools, and determining what information to retain.\" That is why a single augmented LLM already handles a great deal without orchestration, and why the recommendation is to invest in the interface — clear tool descriptions, good retrieval, a defined memory store — before adding patterns. It also marks the concept's boundary: the block describes capability, while workflows and agents describe who owns the control flow around it.",
w:"B — code-decided retrieval and invocation is a legitimate design but describes a workflow step, not the augmented-LLM block, whose whole point is that the model chooses. C — retrieval, tools and memory are runtime augmentations supplied per request; nothing is baked into weights. D — the augmented LLM is the building block of *all* the patterns, including a single call with no other agents present."},

{id:1702,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A content API must screen every user prompt for policy violations while simultaneously generating the response, so that the response is discarded if the screen fails. The two calls do not depend on each other. Which pattern is this?",
o:["A. Evaluator-optimiser","B. Parallelisation by sectioning","C. Prompt chaining","D. Orchestrator-workers"],a:1,
e:"**Sectioning** is the parallelisation variant that breaks a task \"into independent subtasks run in parallel.\" Anthropic gives this exact example: \"implementing guardrails where one model instance processes user queries while another screens them for inappropriate content.\" The two lanes are fixed in code, need no output from each other, and are merged by your logic — discard the response if the screen fails. The benefit is latency: the guardrail is effectively free in wall-clock terms, whereas screening first and then generating would serialise the two.",
w:"A — there is no critique-and-revise loop; the screen does not feed back into a regenerated response. C — chaining would run the screen and the generation in sequence, which is the design being avoided. D — no LLM plans the split; both lanes are constants in the code, so there is no orchestrator."},

{id:1703,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Sending straightforward questions to a smaller, faster model and difficult or unusual questions to a more capable one is, in Anthropic's taxonomy, an example of which pattern?",
o:["A. Parallelisation, voting variant","B. Evaluator-optimiser","C. Routing","D. Orchestrator-workers"],a:2,
e:"**Routing** \"classifies an input and directs it to a specialized followup task,\" and Anthropic lists cost-based model selection as a canonical example — \"routing easy/common questions to smaller models... and hard/unusual questions to more capable models... to optimize cost and speed.\" The classifier can be an LLM call or ordinary code. What makes it routing is that a **fixed, known set of destinations** exists and exactly one is chosen per input. Two details matter in practice: a default destination for inputs no class fits, and monitoring of the class distribution so drift is visible.",
w:"A — voting runs the same task repeatedly and aggregates; here each input takes one path. B — nothing is critiqued and regenerated; the chosen model answers once. D — no subtasks are created at runtime and there is no synthesis step; a single destination handles the whole input."},

{id:1704,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A report generator first asks the model to produce a section outline, validates that the outline covers the required headings, and then asks the model to write each section against that outline. Which pattern is this?",
o:["A. Routing, because the outline determines which section writer is used","B. Parallelisation by sectioning, because the outline divides the work","C. Orchestrator-workers, because the outline is a runtime plan","D. Prompt chaining, because each call consumes the previous call's output through a programmatic check"],a:3,
e:"**Prompt chaining** \"decomposes a task into a sequence of steps, where each LLM call processes the output of the previous one,\" optionally with programmatic checks between steps — Anthropic's own example is \"writing an outline of a document, checking that the outline meets certain criteria, then writing the document based on the outline.\" The tell is a *fixed* sequence with a validation gate, not a plan whose shape varies. Even though the outline is data produced at runtime, the number and order of stages is a constant in your code, which is why this is chaining rather than orchestrator-workers.",
w:"A — routing selects one of several known destinations for an input; here every input traverses the same stages. B — the section writes could later be parallelised, but the pattern described is the sequential outline-then-write dependency with a check between. C — the orchestrator-workers signature is a subtask *set* you could not predict; here the stages are fixed and only their content varies."},

{id:1705,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team is asked to \"classify support emails into five categories and store the category.\" They design a five-step prompt chain with a router, an orchestrator and a self-critique loop. Accuracy in testing is no better than a single classification call, and latency is six times worse. What principle did they violate?",
o:["A. Find the simplest solution that works and add complexity only when it demonstrably helps","B. Every classification task requires a voting ensemble to be production-ready","C. Orchestration patterns must not be combined within one system","D. Classification should always be implemented as routing rather than as a single call"],a:0,
e:"Anthropic's closing advice on the patterns is to \"find the simplest solution possible, and only increase complexity when needed\" — and to remember that \"agentic systems often trade latency and cost for better task performance,\" a trade only worth making when performance actually improves. A five-category label with a known taxonomy is a single augmented-LLM call, optionally with native structured output to guarantee the label is one of the five. Measure first: if the elaborate design does not beat the simple one on your eval set, it is pure cost, added latency and extra failure surface.",
w:"B — voting costs N× and only helps when attempt errors are substantially independent; it is not a default requirement. C — combining patterns is common and often correct; the fault here is that none of them was needed. D — routing dispatches to specialised downstream handlers, and calling it routing does not change that one call suffices to produce a label."},

{id:1706,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A coding assistant must implement a feature request that will touch an unpredictable number of files — which files, and how many, is only discoverable by reading the codebase. Which pattern does Anthropic's taxonomy point to, and what must the implementation include?",
o:["A. Parallelisation by sectioning, with one worker per top-level directory and a merged diff","B. Orchestrator-workers, with a central LLM that plans the file-level subtasks at runtime, delegates them and synthesises the results","C. Routing, with one specialised worker per file type selected by extension","D. Prompt chaining, with one stage per file discovered during the first stage"],a:1,
e:"Orchestrator-workers is \"well-suited for complex tasks where you can't predict the subtasks needed,\" and Anthropic's example is exactly \"coding products that make complex changes to multiple files each time.\" The obligatory parts are the **runtime plan** (the orchestrator decides which files and what change each needs), **delegation** to workers with clear boundaries so they do not overlap, and a **synthesis** step that reconciles the returns into one coherent change. It remains a workflow: the plan/delegate/synthesise path is your code, and only the subtask list is dynamic.",
w:"A — sectioning requires boundaries fixed in advance; directories are a proxy that will both miss relevant files and spawn empty workers. C — routing picks one destination per input, which cannot express a change spanning several files at once. D — a chain has a fixed stage count; generating stages from step 1's findings is a runtime decomposition, which is orchestrator-workers under another name."},

{id:1707,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team must build a system that, given a failing test in an unfamiliar repository, finds and fixes the cause. The number of steps, the files involved and the tools needed are all unknown until the work is under way, and the test suite provides feedback after every attempt. Which choice does Anthropic's guidance support?",
o:["A. Prompt chaining, with a stage for locate, a stage for fix and a stage for verify","B. Parallelisation by voting, with several candidate fixes generated and the majority applied","C. An agent, because the problem is open-ended and the environment supplies ground-truth feedback each turn","D. Routing, with a classifier that selects a repair strategy by failure type"],a:2,
e:"Agents are for \"open-ended problems where it's difficult or impossible to predict the required number of steps, and where you can't hardcode a fixed path.\" The other precondition is present too: agents \"gain ground truth from the environment at each step\" — here the test suite. That objective signal is what keeps a self-directed loop honest, and it is why SWE-bench-style repair is Anthropic's own example. The obligations that come with the choice are explicit: sandboxed testing, appropriate guardrails, a bounded number of iterations, and acceptance of higher cost and compounding errors.",
w:"A — a three-stage chain cannot express \"locate\" taking eleven exploratory tool calls in one repository and two in another; the step count is the thing that varies. B — voting on whole candidate fixes is wasteful and unsound here, since correctness is decided by the test suite rather than by agreement between attempts. D — a strategy classifier presumes a known taxonomy of failures and fixed repair recipes, which an unfamiliar repository denies you."},

{id:1708,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An interactive assistant has a 2-second p95 latency budget. A proposal replaces its single answering call with a three-stage chain — normalise the question, retrieve and re-rank, then answer — which raises answer quality on the eval set from 86% to 91% but pushes p95 to 5.5 seconds. What is the sound engineering response?",
o:["A. Adopt the chain, because accuracy is the primary metric for a question-answering system","B. Adopt the chain and hide the latency by streaming the final stage's tokens as they arrive","C. Reject chaining entirely, since sequential decomposition is unsuitable for interactive systems","D. Keep the single call on the interactive path and use the chain only where the latency budget allows, such as an async or batch path"],a:3,
e:"Chaining explicitly trades **latency for accuracy**, and a latency budget is a requirement, not a preference. Five percentage points bought at 3.5 seconds over budget is a failed design for an interactive path. The correct move is to place the expensive pattern where its cost is affordable: pre-computation, an asynchronous \"deeper answer\" follow-up, or a batch pipeline that populates a cache the interactive path reads. Alternatives worth measuring on the fast path are a better single prompt, cached retrieval, or a cheaper model with a stable prefix cache — none of which serialise three round trips.",
w:"A — accuracy is not the only metric; an assistant that misses its interactive budget is a different, worse product regardless of its eval score. B — streaming improves perceived time-to-first-token but does nothing for the two stages that must complete before generation begins. C — the pattern is fine and useful; what fails is applying it on a path that cannot afford three sequential calls."},

{id:1709,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"Why is a workflow generally easier to test than an agent built for the same task?",
o:["A. Because each step has a fixed position in the path, so it can be exercised in isolation with known inputs and asserted on directly","B. Because the LLM calls inside a workflow are deterministic, so their outputs can be compared exactly","C. Because workflows make no tool calls, so no mocking is required","D. Because workflows are single-call systems and therefore have only one thing to test"],a:0,
e:"Testability follows from **fixed structure**, not from determinism. In a workflow you know stage 3 always receives stage 2's output, so you can feed stage 3 curated inputs, assert on its output shape and content, and test each gate's failure branch — plus an end-to-end test over the whole fixed path. An agent's control flow varies per run, so there is no stable unit boundary and you are pushed towards trajectory-level evaluation and property-based assertions. The LLM steps remain non-deterministic in both cases, which is why workflow step tests assert on properties and invariants rather than exact strings.",
w:"B — model calls are not deterministic; output can vary run to run, and the deprecation of `temperature` on current models does not change that. C — workflows routinely call tools and retrieval, so test doubles are just as necessary. D — a workflow is a multi-step composition by definition; a single call is the augmented-LLM building block, not a workflow."},

{id:1710,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A team claims their workflow-based pipeline is \"deterministic, unlike an agent.\" What is the precise correction?",
o:["A. Neither is deterministic, because the order of steps in a workflow is chosen by the model at runtime","B. The control flow is deterministic — the same path runs every time — but each LLM step's output is not, so the pipeline as a whole is not reproducible","C. Both are deterministic provided the model ID is pinned to a snapshot, since pinned weights produce identical output","D. The workflow is fully deterministic, because programmatic gates between steps remove variance from the model outputs"],a:1,
e:"The claim is half true. A workflow's **path** is fixed by your code: the same stages run in the same order for every input, which is what buys auditability, capacity planning and step-level testing. The **content** each stage produces is still a sample from a model and can differ between identical runs. Current model IDs are dateless pinned snapshots with fixed weights, but the serving infrastructure can change and output is not bit-identical even so — pinning buys stability, not reproducibility. Design accordingly: assert on invariants, not exact strings, and log actual outputs rather than assuming you can regenerate them.",
w:"A — step order in a workflow is written in code, not chosen by the model; that is the definition of a workflow. C — pinning fixes the weights but not the serving stack, and sampling remains non-deterministic, so identical output is not guaranteed. D — gates *reject* outputs that violate a constraint, narrowing the space of accepted values without making the accepted value unique."},

{id:1711,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A routing workflow classifies each inbound message into one of four known intents and dispatches accordingly. In production about 3% of messages are classified with visibly low confidence and the handler they land in produces nonsense. What is the appropriate change to the pattern?",
o:["A. Remove the classifier and send every message to all four handlers, then pick the best output","B. Replace routing with an autonomous agent so it can decide what to do with unfamiliar messages","C. Add an explicit fallback destination — a general handler or a human queue — and route low-confidence or unmatched inputs there","D. Retrain the classifier prompt until confidence exceeds the threshold on all messages"],a:2,
e:"A router is only as good as its **destination coverage**, and real traffic always contains inputs that fit no class. The standard fix is a designed **default route**: a general-purpose handler, a clarification turn, or a human queue, selected by a confidence threshold or by an explicit \"other\" class the classifier may emit. Two supporting practices make it work: log the class distribution and the fallback rate so new intents surface as drift, and treat a rising fallback rate as the trigger to add a route rather than as noise. Silent misrouting is the failure mode worth engineering against.",
w:"A — fanning out to all four multiplies cost on every message and still needs a selection rule, which is the judgement the classifier was making. B — autonomy does not supply the missing knowledge of what these messages want; a bounded fallback path is cheaper, testable and safer. D — no prompt reaches full confidence on open-world traffic, and chasing that target hides the genuinely out-of-taxonomy inputs instead of handling them."},

{id:1712,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A routing workflow has run stably for a year. Over three months, accuracy on one destination quietly declines and complaints rise, though no code has changed and the classifier's own confidence scores remain high. Which monitoring signal would most likely have surfaced this earliest?",
o:["A. Token cost per request, which rises as inputs become harder to classify","B. Average end-to-end latency per route, which grows when a handler is mismatched","C. The classifier's mean confidence score, tracked as a daily time series","D. The distribution of inputs across routes over time, compared against the handler-level outcome rate"],a:3,
e:"This is **input drift**: the traffic mix changed, so a class that used to be homogeneous now contains a new sub-population the handler was never built for. Confidence tracks the classifier's certainty about its label, not the label's usefulness, so it stays high while the outcome degrades — which makes confidence alone a poor drift alarm. The signal that catches it is the **joint** view: share of traffic per route over time, paired with a per-route outcome measure such as resolution, escalation or complaint rate. A shifted mix with flat confidence and falling outcomes is the diagnostic pattern.",
w:"A — cost per request is dominated by prompt and output size and is largely insensitive to whether the chosen route was appropriate. B — latency reflects handler implementation; a mismatched handler often responds just as fast, confidently and wrongly. C — a confident classifier can be consistently wrong about a new sub-population, which is exactly what the scenario describes."},

{id:1713,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pipeline fans out three \"independent\" sections in parallel: summarise the transcript, extract the action items, and write the follow-up email. Reviewers report the email frequently omits items that the extraction step found. What is the design error?",
o:["A. The email section depends on the extraction section's output, so the three are not independent and sectioning is the wrong pattern for that edge","B. The three calls should have been given the same system prompt to keep them consistent","C. Parallelisation requires an odd number of branches so that ties can be broken","D. The transcript should have been chunked so each branch received a smaller slice"],a:0,
e:"Sectioning's precondition is **genuine independence**: each subtask must be answerable from the shared input alone, with no need for a sibling's output. Running a dependent step in parallel does not make it faster, it makes it uninformed — the email writer is re-deriving action items from the raw transcript and inevitably diverging from the extractor. The fix is to respect the dependency graph: parallelise summary and extraction, then chain the email off the extraction result. Parallelism is licensed by the data dependencies, not by a wish for lower latency.",
w:"B — a shared system prompt does not transmit the extractor's findings; the email branch still never sees them. C — tie-breaking belongs to the voting variant, where branches perform the same task; these branches perform different tasks. D — chunking addresses context size, not the missing dependency, and would give the email writer even less of the transcript."},

{id:1714,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A safety screen for user-generated images uses a three-way vote and blocks content when at least two of three calls flag it. Product reports too many false blocks; trust and safety reports the occasional miss is far more damaging than a false block. Which change to the aggregation policy follows from those stated priorities?",
o:["A. Raise the threshold to unanimity, which reduces false blocks and therefore complaints","B. Keep three calls but block on any single flag, and send the disagreement cases to human review rather than resolving them by vote","C. Reduce to a single call, since a three-way vote that disagrees carries no information","D. Weight each call by its self-reported confidence and block above a tuned weighted score"],a:1,
e:"The aggregation rule is where you encode **asymmetric cost**. If a miss is much worse than a false block, the conservative rule treats any flag as sufficient — maximising sensitivity — and then recovers precision with a cheap human adjudication path on the disagreement set, which by construction is small. Voting's real product here is not a verdict but a **disagreement signal** identifying the ambiguous inputs worth a person's attention. Choose the threshold from the relative cost of the two error types, never from complaint volume alone; unanimity moves the system the wrong way on the error that matters.",
w:"A — unanimity is the least sensitive rule available and increases misses, the error trust and safety called far more damaging. C — the disagreement set is the most informative output of the vote, and dropping to one call also removes the sensitivity gain. D — self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated and does not track the cost asymmetry, so tuning a weighted score adds a fragile knob without addressing which error type is worse."},

{id:1715,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An evaluator-optimiser loop for translation refinement occasionally runs for dozens of rounds, and reviewers cannot tell those runs apart from productive ones. What termination design is appropriate?",
o:["A. Terminate as soon as the evaluator's score exceeds a fixed threshold, and never bound the round count","B. Terminate when the generator produces the same text twice in a row, which proves convergence","C. Bound the rounds, stop early when the evaluator raises no actionable issue, and abandon when a round yields no measurable improvement — recording which condition fired","D. Terminate when the token budget is exhausted, since cost is the only hard constraint"],a:2,
e:"Iterative loops need **three exits and a record of which one fired**: success (no actionable criticism remains), stagnation (a round produced no measurable improvement on the criterion), and a hard cap on rounds or spend as a backstop. Logging the exit reason is what makes runs distinguishable afterwards, and the distribution of exit reasons is the health metric — a rising share of cap-hits means the criteria are unachievable or badly specified, while rising stagnation means the feedback is not actionable. The precondition applies throughout: the pattern needs clear criteria and measurable value from refinement.",
w:"A — an unbounded loop with only a success exit is the failure being described; an unreachable threshold runs forever. B — textual repetition can indicate a stuck loop rather than a good result, and near-identical rewordings never trigger it. D — exhausting the budget on every hard input is the most expensive possible stopping rule and tells you nothing about why the run ended."},

{id:1716,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What most fundamentally separates the evaluator-optimiser pattern from the voting variant of parallelisation?",
o:["A. Evaluator-optimiser uses two different models, while voting uses one model repeatedly","B. Evaluator-optimiser is an agent, while voting is a workflow","C. Evaluator-optimiser is cheaper, because it makes fewer calls","D. Evaluator-optimiser is a sequential feedback loop in which a critique drives the next attempt, while voting runs independent attempts and aggregates them without feedback"],a:3,
e:"The dividing line is **information flow between attempts**. In evaluator-optimiser \"one LLM call generates a response while another provides evaluation and feedback, in a loop\" — attempt N+1 is conditioned on the criticism of attempt N, which is why it can improve a specific weakness. In voting, the attempts never see each other; they are independent samples aggregated by your code, and the value comes from diversity rather than refinement. Both are workflows, both can use one model or several, and neither is reliably cheaper — voting parallelises N calls, refinement serialises an unbounded number.",
w:"A — either pattern can use one model or several; evaluator-optimiser commonly uses the same model in both roles, which is a known weakness rather than a definition. B — both are workflows in Anthropic's taxonomy, since the loop and the aggregation are both written in your code. C — refinement can run many sequential rounds and easily exceed a fixed N-way vote, so cost is not the distinguishing property."},

{id:1717,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which of the following is *not* one of the workflow patterns named in Anthropic's \"Building effective agents\"?",
o:["A. Retrieval-augmented generation","B. Routing","C. Evaluator-optimiser","D. Orchestrator-workers"],a:0,
e:"The named workflows are **prompt chaining, routing, parallelisation (sectioning and voting), orchestrator-workers and evaluator-optimiser**. Retrieval belongs one level down: it is one of the augmentations — with tools and memory — that constitute the **augmented LLM** building block on which all the patterns are built. Keeping the levels straight is practically useful: retrieval is a capability you give a call, whereas a workflow describes how calls are arranged. A single augmented LLM doing retrieval is not a workflow at all, and adding retrieval to any of the five does not change which pattern it is.",
w:"B — routing classifies an input and directs it to one of a fixed set of specialised followup tasks, and is one of the five. C — evaluator-optimiser generates then critiques in a loop, and is one of the five. D — orchestrator-workers plans subtasks at runtime, delegates and synthesises, and is one of the five despite that runtime decomposition."},

{id:1718,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which statement matches Anthropic's definition of an agent, as distinct from a workflow?",
o:["A. A system in which several LLM calls run in parallel and their outputs are combined","B. A system in which the LLM dynamically directs its own process and tool usage, retaining control over how the task is accomplished","C. A system in which an LLM is given tools, regardless of who decides when they are called","D. A system in which an LLM call is repeated until a fixed iteration limit is reached"],a:1,
e:"The definitions turn on **ownership of control flow**. Workflows are \"systems where LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths\"; agents are \"systems where LLMs dynamically direct their own processes and tool usage, maintaining control over how they accomplish tasks.\" Everything else — parallelism, tool availability, iteration, runtime data-dependence — is orthogonal. This is why orchestrator-workers, which decomposes at runtime, is still classified a workflow: your code owns the decompose/delegate/synthesise path, and only its contents vary.",
w:"A — that describes parallelisation, a workflow, and concurrency has no bearing on the distinction. C — having tools is a property of the augmented-LLM building block; every workflow step may have tools while your code still decides the path. D — a bounded loop is a guardrail found in both workflows and agents, and the limit says nothing about who chooses the next step."},

{id:1719,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Anthropic's guidance attaches specific obligations to deploying an autonomous agent rather than a workflow. Which set is accurate?",
o:["A. Guarantee determinism, prohibit parallel tool calls, and cap the context window","B. Fine-tune the model on your task, remove retrieval, and disable memory","C. Accept higher cost and the potential for compounding errors, test extensively in sandboxed environments, and add guardrails","D. Restrict the agent to read-only tools and require that every tool call be approved by a human"],a:2,
e:"The stated conditions are trust plus containment: agents suit open-ended problems, but \"you must have some level of trust in its decision-making,\" and because of \"higher costs, and the potential for compounding errors,\" Anthropic recommends \"extensive testing in sandboxed environments, along with the appropriate guardrails.\" Practical guardrails include bounded iterations, explicit stopping conditions, human checkpoints at consequential steps, and least-privilege tools. Note what is not on the list: determinism is unavailable, and blanket read-only or approve-everything rules would remove the autonomy that motivated the choice.",
w:"A — determinism cannot be guaranteed for any LLM system, parallel tool use is often desirable, and context size is a budget rather than an agent-specific obligation. B — fine-tuning is unrelated, and stripping retrieval and memory removes the augmentations the agent depends on. D — read-only tools and per-call approval suit specific high-impact actions, not a universal rule; applied everywhere they defeat the pattern and cause approval fatigue."},

{id:1720,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team is deciding between a multi-agent architecture and a single agent for two candidate products: (i) a compliance review where every step must reason over the same 200-page policy document and each finding depends on the previous one, and (ii) a market scan that must gather independent facts about 30 competitors from many tools. Which assessment is right?",
o:["A. Both suit multi-agent, since both involve large amounts of information","B. Neither suits multi-agent, since coordination overhead always exceeds the benefit","C. Case (i) suits multi-agent because the document exceeds one context window; case (ii) does not because 30 lookups are trivially cheap","D. Case (ii) suits multi-agent because the subtasks are independent and tool-heavy; case (i) does not because all steps share one context and are mutually dependent"],a:3,
e:"The published criteria are specific. Multi-agent earns its cost on tasks with **heavy parallelisation**, information that **exceeds a single context window**, and **many complex tools**. It is a poor fit where all agents must share the same context or where there are many dependencies between subtasks — coordination and context-sync overhead then dominate, and you also pay a large token multiplier (agents around 4× a chat, multi-agent systems around 15×). The competitor scan is the canonical fit: 30 independent investigations each returning a small distilled record. The sequential single-document review is the canonical anti-fit.",
w:"A — volume alone is not the criterion; a shared 200-page context is precisely the case the guidance excludes, since every agent would need the same material. B — the criteria exist because the benefit does sometimes exceed the overhead, as in the independent, tool-heavy scan. C — this inverts both cases: a document all steps must share argues *against* fan-out, and 30 independent tool-heavy investigations are exactly what parallel subagents are for."},

{id:1721,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A team will adopt an agent framework for a production system. Which requirement should most influence the choice, given Anthropic's stated concern about frameworks?",
o:["A. That the framework lets you inspect the exact prompts and responses it sends and receives","B. That the framework supports the largest number of model providers","C. That the framework's abstractions hide the prompt entirely, so application code never deals with strings","D. That the framework ships the most prebuilt agent templates"],a:0,
e:"The named risk is that abstraction \"can obscure the underlying prompts and responses, making them harder to debug,\" and the named mitigation is to \"ensure you understand the underlying code\" because \"incorrect assumptions about what's under the hood are a common source of customer error.\" As a selection criterion that means demanding **observability of the wire traffic** — a way to dump the actual request, the tool definitions sent, the raw response and the token usage. Everything else you might want from a framework (persistence, state, retries, traces) is unusable in an incident if you cannot see what was actually sent.",
w:"B — provider breadth is a portability nicety and does nothing for debuggability, which is the stated concern. C — hiding the prompt is the failure mode described, not a feature; it is what makes incidents unresolvable. D — templates accelerate the first demo and are exactly the temptation \"to add complexity when a simpler setup would suffice.\""},

{id:1722,d:"D3",s:"Agent Patterns & Frameworks",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Anthropic's parallelisation workflow has two named variants. What are they?",
o:["A. Fan-out and fan-in","B. Sectioning and voting","C. Mapping and reducing","D. Sharding and quorum"],a:1,
e:"The two variants are **sectioning** — \"breaking a task into independent subtasks run in parallel\" — and **voting** — \"running the same task multiple times to get diverse outputs.\" The distinction is what each branch is asked to do: different parts of one job, or the same job repeatedly. It matters because the two have different aggregation logic (merge sections versus apply a decision rule to votes), different cost profiles (sectioning divides the work, voting multiplies it) and different preconditions (sectioning needs genuine independence between parts, voting needs substantially independent errors between attempts).",
w:"A — fan-out and fan-in describe the mechanical shape of any parallel execution and are not the taxonomy's variant names. C — map and reduce are general data-processing stages, whereas sectioning and voting differ in *what* is asked rather than in the presence of a reduce step. D — sharding and quorum come from distributed data systems and do not appear in the taxonomy."},

{id:1723,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An orchestrator agent is supposed to delegate research to subagents, but the implementation has it call the web-search tool itself dozens of times, then spawn subagents only to reformat what it found. The team observes that the orchestrator's context fills long before the task completes. What is the architectural mistake?",
o:["A. The subagents were given too few tools to be useful","B. The orchestrator should have spawned more subagents in parallel","C. The orchestrator is doing the bulk-consuming work itself, so no context isolation occurs — the roles are inverted","D. The orchestrator should have used a larger-context model, which would remove the limit"],a:2,
e:"The whole point of the split is **where the tokens land**. Subagents exist to absorb the high-volume work — searching, reading, filtering — in their own context windows and return a small distilled result; the orchestrator's job is to plan, delegate, reconcile and decide. Here the volume is read into the orchestrator, so the architecture pays multi-agent coordination cost while receiving none of the isolation benefit, and it fills the one context that must survive the whole run. The rule of thumb: the component that must live longest should read the least.",
w:"A — the subagents' tool inventory is not the problem; they are not being asked to do the reading in the first place. B — more reformatting subagents add cost and coordination without moving a single search out of the orchestrator's context. D — a bigger window postpones the same failure and makes each orchestrator turn more expensive, since the whole accumulated history is resent every iteration."},

{id:1724,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team migrates a research feature from one agent to an orchestrator with parallel subagents. Total token spend triples. They need to demonstrate that the migration nonetheless achieved its intended benefit. Which measurement does that?",
o:["A. Total tokens per completed task, which should fall if the architecture is sound","B. The number of tool calls per task, which should fall as subagents specialise","C. The average response length, which should shorten as returns become distilled","D. Orchestrator context size at the end of a run, which should stay roughly flat as the number of subagents and the volume of source material grow"],a:3,
e:"The benefit claimed for subagents is **context isolation**, not efficiency — total spend is expected to rise (agents run around 4× a chat, multi-agent systems around 15×). So the metric that evidences the benefit is the orchestrator's own context growth: if each subagent consumes 60k tokens of sources and returns a 400-token structured summary, the orchestrator's window should grow slowly and sub-linearly in the material examined. Decoupling that curve is what lets the system tackle corpora no single window could hold. Measure what the design actually buys, and state the cost honestly rather than claiming a saving that is not there.",
w:"A — falling total tokens is not a prediction of this architecture; rising spend is the known and accepted cost. B — a fan-out design usually issues *more* tool calls in total, since several subagents search concurrently. C — output length is a formatting property of the final answer and can be held constant by prompt instructions in either architecture."},

{id:1725,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Anthropic's published measurements of token consumption across interaction styles report roughly what relationship?",
o:["A. Agents use around 4× the tokens of a chat interaction, and multi-agent systems around 15×","B. Agents and multi-agent systems use roughly the same tokens as chat, since subagents share one context","C. Multi-agent systems use about half the tokens of a single agent, because each subagent sees less material","D. Multi-agent systems use around 100× a chat interaction, which makes them uneconomic outside research"],a:0,
e:"The reported figures are that \"agents typically use about 4× more tokens than chat interactions, and multi-agent systems use about 15× more tokens than chats.\" This is the honest economics of the pattern: fan-out buys parallel exploration and context isolation and pays for them in tokens, since each subagent carries its own system prompt and accumulated history and the orchestrator pays again for the returns and the synthesis. The design consequence is a value threshold — multi-agent architectures are justified on tasks valuable enough to absorb the multiplier. **Currency note:** treat these as order-of-magnitude figures, not constants.",
w:"B — subagents deliberately do *not* share one context; separate windows are the mechanism, and they are what multiplies the spend. C — the multiplier runs upward, not downward; each subagent adds its own overhead rather than dividing a fixed budget. D — the published figure is about 15× for multi-agent systems, and such systems are in production rather than confined to research."},

{id:1726,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"An orchestrator is asked to fan out four subtasks in parallel: (1) fetch the customer's order history, (2) fetch the courier's tracking events, (3) decide, from the order history, which shipment is disputed, and (4) draft a reply. Which grouping is correct?",
o:["A. All four can run in parallel, since each has its own tools","B. Subtasks 1 and 2 can run in parallel; 3 depends on 1, and 4 depends on 3","C. Subtasks 1, 2 and 3 can run in parallel; only 4 must wait","D. None can run in parallel, since all four concern the same customer"],a:1,
e:"Parallelism is licensed by the **dependency graph**, not by the availability of separate tools or the desire for speed. Two independent fetches can go concurrently. The disputed-shipment decision consumes the order history, and the draft consumes that decision, so both are sequential edges. Running a dependent step concurrently does not speed it up — it starves it of input and produces a confidently wrong answer that costs more to detect than the latency it saved. Write the dependencies down before choosing the fan-out, and chain any subtask that needs a sibling's result.",
w:"A — separate tools do not remove data dependencies; step 3 cannot decide from history it has not received. C — step 3's input is step 1's output, so it cannot begin until step 1 returns. D — sharing a subject does not create a dependency; the two fetches read different sources and neither needs the other."},

{id:1727,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A three-level hierarchy — orchestrator, team leads, workers — is used for report generation. The orchestrator asks for \"a risk assessment of our top three suppliers, focused on delivery reliability.\" A worker returns a detailed analysis of one supplier's *credit* risk. Nothing errored, and every prompt in the chain was well formed. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. The worker's context window overflowed, so it lost the original request","B. The worker's tools were misconfigured and returned credit data instead of delivery data","C. Intent degraded as each level re-described the task in its own words, so the delivery-reliability constraint was lost before it reached the worker","D. The orchestrator should have used a more capable model tier for the workers"],a:2,
e:"Every delegation hop is a **lossy paraphrase**. A lead reading \"risk assessment... focused on delivery reliability\" writes its own worker brief, and the qualifier carrying the whole point is the easiest thing to drop; the worker then executes a well-formed but wrong task. This is the compounding cost that makes deep hierarchies a liability: intent decays multiplicatively with depth while cost, latency and traceability all worsen. Mitigations are to keep the tree shallow, pass the original request verbatim alongside the derived subtask, and require each brief to state its objective, output format, tool guidance and boundaries explicitly.",
w:"A — overflow announces itself with a `model_context_window_exceeded` stop reason or visibly truncated behaviour, and the brief here was small. B — a tool returning the wrong domain of data would show up across many runs and would not produce a coherent credit-risk analysis on request. D — a stronger model executes the brief it was given more competently; it cannot recover a constraint that was never transmitted."},

{id:1728,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"In a two-level design (orchestrator plus workers), a worker discovers that its assigned subtask is really five subtasks. The team is considering letting workers spawn their own workers. What is the better policy?",
o:["A. Allow unlimited nesting but cap total agents, since depth is only a cost concern","B. Allow one extra level for workers, since a three-level tree is still shallow","C. Forbid nesting and require the worker to complete all five parts itself within its own context","D. Have the worker return a structured \"needs decomposition\" result with what it found, and let the orchestrator re-plan and dispatch the new subtasks itself"],a:3,
e:"Keeping the tree **shallow with a single planning authority** preserves what makes the architecture manageable: one component knows the whole plan, budget and progress; every subtask traces to one delegation; and intent is paraphrased once rather than repeatedly. Returning a typed \"needs decomposition\" outcome turns an unexpected discovery into ordinary orchestrator input — it re-plans with full context, deduplicates against work already dispatched, and can decide the extra work is not worth its budget. Depth is not merely a cost concern: it degrades intent, fragments accountability, and makes cost attribution and tracing much harder.",
w:"A — an agent cap bounds spend but not intent loss, latency compounding, or the loss of traceability that depth causes. B — one extra level sounds modest but installs a second planning authority the orchestrator cannot see, budget for, or reconcile. C — forcing one worker through five subtasks reintroduces the context pressure and serialisation the fan-out existed to avoid, and it hides the re-plan from the orchestrator."},

{id:1729,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Three research subagents return findings about a supplier's financial health. Two say \"stable\", one says \"deteriorating\". The orchestrator has no way to tell which to believe. What change to the subagent contract fixes this class of problem?",
o:["A. Require each finding to carry its source identifier, the date of the evidence and the subagent's confidence, in a fixed schema","B. Require each subagent to return a longer narrative so the orchestrator can judge the reasoning","C. Require the orchestrator to re-run all three subagents and take the majority answer","D. Require subagents to agree with each other before returning, by sharing a scratchpad"],a:0,
e:"Conflicts between subagents are inevitable and are not themselves a defect — being unable to **adjudicate** them is. A structured return carrying **provenance** (which document, which URL, which system, as of when) and a **confidence** value turns an unresolvable clash of assertions into a comparable pair of claims: a 2019 filing versus last month's covenant breach is no longer a tie. Enforce the shape rather than requesting it, using native structured output (`output_config.format` with a `json_schema`) or a `strict` tool, so the orchestrator can rely on the fields being present.",
w:"B — longer prose is harder to reconcile, not easier; unstructured narratives are the usual cause of self-contradicting syntheses. C — re-running correlated subagents mostly reproduces the same split at three times the cost and still supplies no evidence to weigh. D — forcing consensus before return destroys the disagreement signal and manufactures a false unanimity that hides the deteriorating finding."},

{id:1730,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Subagent returns now include a `confidence` field. What is the appropriate way for the orchestrator to use it?",
o:["A. Discard any finding below a fixed confidence value, keeping only high-confidence claims","B. Use it to decide where to seek corroboration or escalate, while treating it as the model's self-report rather than a calibrated probability","C. Multiply the confidences of all findings to produce an overall confidence for the report","D. Pass it through to the end user as the report's accuracy percentage"],a:1,
e:"A self-reported confidence is a **routing signal, not a measurement**. Language models are not reliably calibrated, so the useful reading is ordinal and local: low confidence on a load-bearing claim means fetch a second source, dispatch a verification subtask, or flag it for the human reviewer; two conflicting high-confidence claims mean the reconciliation step must surface both. What it must not become is an arithmetic accuracy figure or a silent filter. Pair it with provenance so the orchestrator weighs evidence rather than assertions.",
w:"A — silently dropping low-confidence findings destroys exactly the material a reviewer needs, and low confidence is often attached to the most consequential claim. C — multiplying uncalibrated self-reports has no probabilistic meaning and produces a number that falls towards zero as reports get longer. D — presenting a self-report as an accuracy percentage misleads users and invites reliance the figure cannot support."},

{id:1731,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An orchestrator's synthesis step is instructed to \"produce one coherent answer\" from subagent returns. When two subagents conflict, it silently picks one and writes a fluent paragraph. Reviewers only discover the discarded finding weeks later. What is the correct design change?",
o:["A. Instruct the synthesis prompt to be more careful about contradictions","B. Increase the synthesis step's effort setting so it reasons longer over the returns","C. Add an explicit reconciliation step that detects conflicting claims programmatically, and requires the conflict to be resolved with evidence or escalated and reported rather than absorbed","D. Have the orchestrator average the conflicting values to a middle estimate"],a:2,
e:"\"Produce one coherent answer\" is an instruction to hide disagreement, and a fluent model will comply. Reconciliation must be a **distinct, inspectable step**: with structured, provenance-bearing returns, code can detect that two findings assert different values for the same field, then apply a defined policy — prefer the more recent or more authoritative source, dispatch a tie-break verification subtask, or attach the conflict to the output and escalate. Coherence is an output property you should never buy by discarding evidence, and conflict detection belongs in code because a model asked for coherence will smooth it away.",
w:"A — a prompt asking for care competes with the instruction asking for coherence, and neither is enforceable; the model is not a control. B — more reasoning produces a better-argued single choice, still made silently and still unrecorded. D — averaging fabricates a value neither subagent found, which is worse than choosing one and destroys the provenance trail."},

{id:1732,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team wants a research subagent to be unable to write to the database, while the orchestrator retains write access. How is that separation actually enforced?",
o:["A. By instructing the subagent in its system prompt that it must not write to the database","B. By omitting write examples from the subagent's prompt so it never learns the pattern","C. By having the orchestrator review each write the subagent proposes before it executes","D. By giving each subagent its own tool permission set, so the write tool is not among the tools it can invoke"],a:3,
e:"Least privilege is a property of the **tool inventory and the execution layer**, not of instructions. Subagents are configured with their own context, their own system prompt and their own **tool permissions**, so a read-only researcher simply has no write tool to call — the capability is absent rather than discouraged. This matters because the model is not a security boundary: prompt-level prohibitions are advisory and can be talked past, especially by injected instructions arriving in retrieved content. Scope each subagent to the narrowest tool set that does its job, and keep privileged actions with the component you audit most closely.",
w:"A — system-prompt prohibitions are advisory; they bound behaviour on average and provide no guarantee under adversarial or injected input. B — withholding examples does not remove a capability, and a tool that is present can be invoked from its schema alone. C — an orchestrator review step is a useful pattern but it is another model in the loop, not an enforcement boundary, and it is unnecessary if the tool is simply absent."},

{id:1733,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Four subagent configurations are proposed. Which one is the dangerous combination that should not ship?",
o:["A. A subagent that fetches arbitrary web pages, can read the internal customer database, and can send outbound email","B. A subagent that fetches arbitrary web pages and returns extracted text, with no other tools","C. A subagent that reads the internal customer database and returns records to the orchestrator, with no network tools","D. A subagent that sends outbound email using arguments the orchestrator supplies, with no read tools"],a:0,
e:"The hazardous configuration holds all three of **untrusted input, sensitive data and an outbound channel** in a single trust domain. Fetched web content can carry **indirect prompt injection**; if that same component can read customer records and send email, an injected instruction has everything it needs to exfiltrate, and no prompt-level defence closes it because the model is not a security boundary. The fix is to separate trust domains: the fetcher reads untrusted content and holds no sensitive access or egress, and privileged actions live in components whose inputs are not attacker-controlled.",
w:"B — a fetcher exposed to injection but holding neither sensitive data nor an outbound channel has a bounded blast radius; the worst case is a poisoned finding, which provenance and reconciliation can surface. C — a database reader whose inputs come from the orchestrator rather than the open web is not reachable by injected content and cannot exfiltrate without an egress tool. D — a send-only component with no read access and no untrusted input has nothing sensitive to leak and no channel by which instructions reach it."},

{id:1734,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An operations agent can issue account credits. Finance wants throughput but will not accept unreviewed large credits. Which authority model best fits?",
o:["A. Require human approval for every credit, so no unreviewed credit is possible","B. Graduate authority by impact: auto-issue below a small threshold, single approver in a middle band, two approvers above it, with the thresholds and the tiering enforced in code","C. Let the agent issue any credit and have a nightly report list them for retrospective review","D. Let the agent decide when a credit is large enough to warrant approval, guided by its system prompt"],a:1,
e:"**Graduated authority** matches the strength of the control to the consequence of the action, which is what lets a system be both fast and safe. Small, reversible, high-volume actions run unattended; the middle band gets one reviewer; the top band gets dual control. Two properties are essential: the thresholds are evaluated in **deterministic code** against the authenticated session rather than asserted by the model, and every tier's decision is logged. This is human-in-the-loop done well — the model proposes, code authorises — and it avoids both unreviewed large actions and approval fatigue on trivial ones.",
w:"A — gating everything is the classic route to approval fatigue: reviewers rubber-stamp a queue of trivial credits and stop reading the one that matters. C — retrospective review does not prevent the harm it discovers, and credits are hard to unwind once the customer has been notified. D — a model-chosen threshold is advisory and can be reasoned around; the limit must be an enforced check, not an instruction."},

{id:1735,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A deployment agent proposes infrastructure changes. The team wants human oversight without stalling routine work. Which arrangement gives the strongest guarantee that a human saw what was actually applied?",
o:["A. The agent explains in prose what it intends to do, a human replies \"approved\", and the agent then performs the change","B. The agent performs the change and posts a summary to a channel a human monitors","C. The agent emits a concrete machine-readable plan (the exact diff or API calls), the human approves that artefact, and deterministic code applies precisely the approved artefact","D. The agent asks for approval, and on approval re-plans with fresh information before applying the change"],a:2,
e:"The gap in most approval flows is between **what was approved and what was executed**. Binding them means the approval target is an artefact — a diff, a rendered plan, an explicit list of API calls — and the executor is ordinary code that applies that artefact and nothing else. Then the human's consent covers the actual mutation, the artefact is the audit record, and a model that changes its mind after approval cannot act on it. Prose intent followed by a second model-driven execution pass leaves the model free to do something adjacent to what was described, which is precisely what this control exists to prevent.",
w:"A — the prose description and the subsequent action are separate model outputs, so the human approved a summary rather than the change, and drift between them is unverifiable. B — after-the-fact notification is monitoring, not oversight; the change is already live. D — re-planning after approval voids the approval, since what gets applied is a plan no human ever saw."},

{id:1736,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An internal agent was launched with a human approval prompt on every tool call. Six weeks later, telemetry shows a median approval latency of 1.2 seconds, an approval rate of 99.6%, and two incidents where reviewers approved clearly wrong destructive actions. What is the diagnosis and the fix?",
o:["A. Reviewers are undertrained; add documentation and a quiz before granting approval rights","B. The approval UI is too slow to read; add a summary line to each prompt","C. The incidents are unrelated to gating; add a second reviewer to every approval","D. Over-gating has produced approval fatigue; auto-approve the low-impact majority and reserve prompts for consequential, irreversible actions with enough context to judge"],a:3,
e:"A 99.6% approval rate at 1.2 seconds is the signature of **approval fatigue**: the gate has become a keystroke, so it no longer carries information and the rare dangerous request is waved through with the rest. Over-gating actively reduces safety while adding latency. The remedy is selectivity — classify actions by impact and reversibility, let the routine majority run unattended under logging and hard caps, and reserve interruption for actions worth stopping for, presented with the detail a reviewer needs. Monitor the approval rate as a health metric: approaching 100% means the gate is decorative.",
w:"A — the reviewers' problem is volume and monotony, not knowledge; trained reviewers facing hundreds of trivial prompts behave the same way. B — a better summary on each of hundreds of prompts still asks for hundreds of decisions per day, and the count is the defect. C — doubling the reviewers on a fatigued queue doubles the rubber-stamping and the latency."},

{id:1737,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent with shell and filesystem tools already runs in a container whose filesystem is restricted to a scratch working directory. A reviewer says the sandbox is still materially incomplete. What is the most important remaining restriction?",
o:["A. Restrict outbound network access to an explicit allowlist, so the agent cannot reach arbitrary hosts","B. Restrict the agent's maximum output tokens so it cannot generate large scripts","C. Restrict which model the agent may call, to prevent unapproved model use","D. Restrict the container's CPU share so runaway processes cannot saturate the host"],a:0,
e:"Filesystem confinement addresses damage to the host; it does nothing about **egress**. A shell with unrestricted network access can fetch and execute arbitrary code, reach internal services the container can route to, and post data outward — so a prompt injection in a file the agent reads becomes both a remote-code-execution and an exfiltration path. An egress allowlist, plus no ambient credentials mounted into the container and an ephemeral filesystem, is what makes the sandbox meaningful. Resource limits matter for stability but bound noise rather than the security blast radius.",
w:"B — output length limits control cost and verbosity, and a short command is quite sufficient to do harm. C — model governance is a procurement and policy concern and does not constrain what the shell can reach. D — CPU limits protect availability and are worth having, but a fully throttled container with open egress can still exfiltrate data and pull in hostile code."},

{id:1738,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A CI agent needs to run tests and open pull requests. The current design mounts the team's long-lived deployment credentials into the agent's container \"so it has whatever it needs\". What is the concrete risk and the better arrangement?",
o:["A. The risk is credential expiry mid-run; rotate the credentials on a fixed schedule","B. The risk is that any injected instruction or compromised dependency inherits full deployment authority; issue a short-lived, narrowly scoped credential for the specific repository actions, injected by the harness rather than reachable by the agent's shell","C. The risk is token cost, since credentials occupy context; store them in a file instead of the system prompt","D. The risk is auditability; add a log line whenever the agent uses the credential"],a:1,
e:"Mounting broad, long-lived credentials into a sandbox means the **blast radius equals the credential's scope**, and an agent that reads untrusted content (issue text, dependency code, fetched pages) is reachable by injection. Least privilege for agents is credential-shaped as well as tool-shaped: short-lived tokens, scoped to the exact operations needed, obtained by the surrounding harness and used on the agent's behalf rather than handed to a shell it controls. Authorise against the authenticated session and the declared scope, never against what the agent asserts it needs.",
w:"A — scheduled rotation shortens the window slightly but leaves a fully scoped credential available during every run. C — credentials in context is a separate mistake, and the cost framing misses that the danger is authority, not tokens. D — logging tells you afterwards what was done with full deployment rights; it does not reduce what could be done."},

{id:1739,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In an agent's memory architecture, which split between in-context working memory and external durable memory is sound?",
o:["A. Everything the agent has ever learned belongs in context, since retrieval adds latency","B. Working memory holds durable user facts, while external memory holds the current turn's tool results","C. Working memory holds what the current step needs — recent turns, active tool results, the goal; durable memory holds facts and artefacts that must outlive the session, retrieved on demand","D. Durable memory should hold the full transcript of every session, which is replayed into context at the start of the next one"],a:2,
e:"The two stores answer different questions. **Working memory** is the request itself — accumulated messages, the goal, the tool results the next decision depends on — bounded by the context window and re-sent in full on every iteration, so its size is a per-turn cost. **Durable memory** is an external store (files, a database, a vector index) holding stable preferences, decisions, produced artefacts and progress notes, read selectively when relevant. Getting the split wrong shows up as either amnesia between sessions or a context window consumed by history the current step does not need.",
w:"A — context is finite and is re-transmitted every iteration, so hoarding everything raises cost per turn and eventually exceeds the window. B — this reverses the roles: transient tool results are exactly what should not be persisted, and durable user facts are exactly what should. D — replaying whole transcripts reintroduces the context pressure durable memory exists to relieve; persist distilled state, not raw history."},

{id:1740,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent works with a user across sessions days apart and must resume without re-asking what was already settled. Which mechanism delivers continuity?",
o:["A. Rely on prompt caching, since the cached prefix preserves the earlier conversation","B. Keep the process alive between sessions so the in-memory conversation object survives","C. Raise `max_tokens` so later requests can carry more of the history","D. At session end, write a distilled state record — decisions, open questions, artefacts, next step — to durable storage; at session start, load it into context"],a:3,
e:"The API is **stateless**: nothing carries over between requests except what you resend, so cross-session continuity is an application responsibility. The working pattern is an explicit **write-back and load** cycle around a compact state record — what was decided, what remains open, where the artefacts live, what the next action is — stored outside the model and injected at the start of the next session. Distil rather than archive: a summary a fresh session can act on beats a transcript that consumes the window. Prompt caching is a cost optimisation over identical prefixes with a short TTL, not a memory system.",
w:"A — caching reduces the price of re-sending an identical prefix, stores nothing semantically, defaults to a five-minute TTL, and would be long cold days later. B — a long-lived process is fragile, does not survive deploys or crashes, and does not scale across instances; durability must be external. C — `max_tokens` caps generated output and has no bearing on what history is available."},

{id:1741,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent's durable memory has accumulated user facts for eight months. It now confidently tells the user their default shipping address is one they moved away from in March, because a note from January says so. What does the memory architecture lack?",
o:["A. Provenance and recency handling — each memory needs a source and a timestamp, with a policy for superseding or invalidating stale entries","B. A larger context window, so all eight months of notes can be considered together","C. A vector index, so retrieval finds the most semantically relevant note","D. A rule forbidding the agent from writing to durable memory without human approval"],a:0,
e:"Durable memory is a **database with no natural expiry**, and write-only accumulation guarantees this failure: an old true statement becomes a current false one. It needs the ordinary discipline of a record store — a timestamp and source per entry, a notion of which field a fact is about so a later value can supersede an earlier one, and explicit invalidation or reconciliation on write. Retrieval should then prefer the current value and be able to surface that it changed. Confident recall of stale facts is more damaging than forgetting, because the user has no cue to doubt it.",
w:"B — a bigger window lets the agent see both notes and still gives it no rule for preferring one, while raising cost per turn. C — semantic similarity ranks the January note highly precisely because it is on-topic; relevance is not recency. D — human approval on writes slows every interaction and does not help, since the January write was correct when it was made."},

{id:1742,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent halts and hands off to a human when it cannot proceed. Which hand-off content is most useful to the person picking it up?",
o:["A. The full untruncated transcript of every model turn and tool result, so nothing is withheld","B. The goal, what has been established and where the evidence sits, the specific blocker, what was already attempted and its outcome, and the concrete decision or input now needed","C. A short apology, the task identifier, and a request that the human restart the task","D. The agent's best guess at the answer, with a note that it is unverified"],a:1,
e:"A hand-off is a **resumption package**, and its quality is measured by whether the human can act without redoing the work. That requires state (the goal and what is settled, with pointers to artefacts and sources), the precise blocker, the attempts already made so they are not repeated, and an explicit ask — the decision, credential or clarification that unblocks it. Include the run identifier and a link to the full trajectory for anyone who wants depth, but lead with the distillation. Dumping a transcript transfers the reading cost to the human; guessing invites reliance on an answer the agent could not stand behind.",
w:"A — an unfiltered transcript is a log, not a hand-off; it makes the human reconstruct state the agent already knew, and it should be linked rather than pasted. C — a restart request discards all progress and repeats the same failure, since the blocker is unchanged. D — an unverified guess presented as output is the failure mode a hand-off exists to avoid, and it obscures what the human actually needs to supply."},

{id:1743,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A production agent logs a termination reason for every run. Over a month the distribution shifts: `clarification_needed` rises from 4% to 27%, while `max_iterations` and `tool_error` stay flat. Which conclusion is best supported?",
o:["A. The model has degraded and should be swapped for a different tier","B. The iteration cap is too low for the new traffic and should be raised","C. The incoming requests have become more ambiguous or have drifted outside what the agent is scoped and equipped to handle","D. The tool descriptions have broken, since more runs now end without a result"],a:2,
e:"The value of a **termination-reason distribution** is that it separates failure modes that all look like \"the agent didn't finish\". Flat `tool_error` says the tools are working; flat `max_iterations` says runs are not thrashing or looping. A specific rise in `clarification_needed` therefore points at the **input side**: new request types, a new entry point sending different traffic, or genuine ambiguity the agent is correctly refusing to guess through. The follow-up is to sample those runs and read the clarifying questions — they name the missing information, telling you whether to extend scope, add a tool, or fix an upstream form.",
w:"A — a model change would be expected to move several reasons at once, and asking for clarification is desired behaviour under ambiguity rather than a defect. B — the cap is not being hit; `max_iterations` is flat, so raising it changes nothing. D — broken tool descriptions typically show up as wrong or failed tool calls and a rising `tool_error` rate, which is explicitly flat here."},

{id:1744,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Beyond checking the final output, what should an agent evaluation measure about the trajectory?",
o:["A. The total wall-clock time, which is the only trajectory property that affects users","B. The number of tokens in the final answer, as a proxy for effort","C. Whether the model's stated reasoning reads as plausible to a human grader","D. Tool-call efficiency and redundancy, whether the run converged or wandered, and whether the path was appropriate to the request"],a:3,
e:"Two runs can produce the same correct answer while being very different systems. **Trajectory evaluation** looks at how the answer was reached: how many tool calls were needed against a reasonable baseline, how many were repeats or near-duplicates, whether the run converged or oscillated between the same states, whether error recovery was clean, and where it terminated. These are the metrics that predict cost, latency and reliability at scale, and they localise defects output-only scoring hides — a redundant search loop, a tool whose description invites misuse, a missing capability the agent works around expensively.",
w:"A — elapsed time is worth tracking but aggregates everything into one number and cannot distinguish a slow tool from a wandering path. B — answer length measures verbosity, is trivially manipulated by formatting instructions, and says nothing about the work done. C — a plausible-sounding rationale is not evidence that the path taken was efficient, or even that it matches what the agent actually did."},

{id:1745,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent answers a data question correctly, but its trajectory shows 31 tool calls, of which 22 are near-identical queries against the same table with slightly different filters. Output-based evaluation passes it. What should the evaluation catch, and how?",
o:["A. The redundancy: assert on trajectory properties such as tool-call count against a baseline and the rate of near-duplicate calls, and fail runs that exceed them","B. Nothing: the answer was correct, which is the only outcome that matters to the user","C. The redundancy, by lowering the iteration cap until such runs fail on the cap","D. The redundancy, by instructing the agent in its system prompt not to repeat queries"],a:0,
e:"Correct-but-wasteful is a real defect: it multiplies cost and latency and usually indicates a fixable cause — a tool whose description does not explain how to filter, a result format that hides what was already retrieved, or missing feedback that the previous call answered the question. Making it **visible** is the first job, so the eval asserts on the trajectory: calls per task against a known-good baseline, near-duplicate call rate, and time-to-first-useful-result. Then fix the cause. Evaluate the path as well as the destination, or you will ship an agent that is right and unaffordable.",
w:"B — the user also experiences the cost and the latency, and a run at three times the necessary call count will not survive scale. C — a lower cap converts a wasteful success into a failure without diagnosing anything, and it will also break legitimately long runs. D — a prompt instruction is a weak, unmeasured mitigation; without a trajectory metric you cannot tell whether it worked."},

{id:1746,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"During testing, an agent receives \"sort out the invoice problem for Acme\" — Acme has four open invoices and three distinct problems. The agent picks one invoice and issues a credit. What behaviour should the test have demanded, and what does that imply for the eval set?",
o:["A. It should have processed all four invoices, and the eval set should include multi-entity requests","B. It should have recognised the request as underspecified and asked a targeted clarifying question before taking any action, and the eval set must contain ambiguous requests with that expected behaviour","C. It should have chosen the largest invoice as the most likely intent, and the eval set should encode that tie-break rule","D. It should have refused the request entirely, and the eval set should treat all ambiguous input as out of scope"],a:1,
e:"Silently resolving ambiguity is the most expensive failure an action-taking agent can make, because it produces a confident, consequential, plausible-looking wrong result. The desired behaviour is to **stop before the irreversible step and ask the narrow question** that disambiguates — which invoice, which problem — and you get it by making ambiguity a first-class eval category with \"asked for clarification and took no action\" as the passing outcome. Real traffic is full of underspecified requests, so an eval set built only from well-formed ones certifies a system that has never met its users.",
w:"A — acting on all four is a guess that multiplies the harm; the request may concern one invoice, and four credits is worse than one. C — a heuristic tie-break hard-codes an assumption about intent and hides the ambiguity behind a rule nobody validated. D — blanket refusal is unhelpful and conflates ambiguity with being out of scope; ambiguity is resolvable by asking."},

{id:1747,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A customer-support agent scoped to billing is asked to recommend a personal investment strategy. It produces a confident, detailed recommendation. What is the desired behaviour and the corresponding test?",
o:["A. Answer as helpfully as possible, since refusing frustrates users, and test that the answer is accurate","B. Route the request to a general-purpose model and test that the hand-off succeeds","C. State that the request is outside its scope, offer the appropriate channel, and take no action — with out-of-scope requests as an explicit eval category","D. Ask a clarifying question about the user's risk tolerance and test that the question is asked"],a:2,
e:"An agent's **scope boundary** has to be as tested as its happy path. The correct response to an out-of-scope request is a clear, non-hedging statement that it cannot help, a pointer to where the user can get help, and no tool calls — and that is only reliable if the boundary is stated in the system prompt *and* exercised by eval cases whose expected outcome is a scoped decline. Confident answering outside scope is a liability, particularly in regulated areas, and unlike a factual error it will not show up in accuracy metrics computed over in-scope questions.",
w:"A — helpfulness inside a defined remit is the goal; answering regulated financial questions from a billing agent creates risk no accuracy score mitigates. B — silently escalating to a general model launders the scope violation and gives the user advice from a system with no mandate for it. D — clarifying questions resolve *ambiguity*; this request is clear and simply outside remit, so more detail about the user does not make it answerable."},

{id:1748,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team writes automated tests for an agent whose correct behaviour on some inputs is to ask a clarifying question rather than answer. Exact-string assertions are hopelessly flaky. What assertion strategy is appropriate?",
o:["A. Pin the model to a snapshot ID so outputs become reproducible and exact strings can be asserted","B. Record one correct response per case and assert cosine similarity above a fixed threshold","C. Retry each test up to five times and pass the case if any attempt matches","D. Assert on observable properties and invariants — that no mutating tool was called, that a question was asked, and that it names the missing information — using a rubric-based or programmatic check"],a:3,
e:"Agent outputs are non-deterministic in wording but should be deterministic in **properties**. So assert the properties: which tools were and were not called (no mutating call on an ambiguous input), the shape of the reply (interrogative, one question, no action taken), and its content (mentions the specific missing field). Tool-call assertions are exact and cheap because they read the trajectory rather than the prose, and the language part can be graded by a rubric check. Pinning a model does not make sampling reproducible, and any strategy that lets a test pass on retry converts a real flake into a hidden one.",
w:"A — current model IDs are already pinned snapshots and output is still not bit-identical, since sampling and serving infrastructure both vary. B — a similarity threshold to one recorded answer is brittle in both directions: it fails valid rephrasings and passes fluent responses that omit the point. C — best-of-five retries change the pass criterion to \"sometimes correct\", which is exactly what you must not certify for an action-taking agent."},

{id:1749,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To \"lose nothing\", an orchestrator appends each subagent's complete message history — every tool call and result — into its own context before synthesising. What is the consequence?",
o:["A. Context isolation is defeated: the orchestrator now carries the aggregate volume, so its window fills and per-iteration cost rises with every subagent","B. Synthesis quality improves, because the orchestrator can audit each subagent's reasoning","C. Total token spend falls, because the subagent summaries no longer need generating","D. Nothing significant changes, since the orchestrator would have read the same material anyway"],a:0,
e:"The architecture's benefit is that the **volume stays in the subagent's window** and only a distilled result crosses the boundary. Re-importing full transcripts inverts that: the orchestrator's context grows with the sum of all subagent work, and because the API is stateless and the whole accumulated history is resent on every iteration, that growth is paid again on each subsequent orchestrator turn. Keep the boundary narrow — a structured result with provenance and confidence — and keep the transcripts in the trace store, where a human or a debugger can read them without occupying the model's window.",
w:"B — more raw material crowds out the reasoning space for synthesis and reliably degrades it; auditing belongs in observability, not in the prompt. C — the summaries are cheap and the transcripts are not, so spend rises substantially. D — a single agent would have read the material serially into one window, which is the limit this architecture exists to escape, and it would not have paid the fan-out overhead as well."},

{id:1750,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What should a subagent's task brief contain, according to Anthropic's published guidance on multi-agent research systems?",
o:["A. The orchestrator's full plan, so the subagent can see how its work fits in","B. An objective, an output format, guidance on the tools and sources to use, and clear task boundaries","C. A minimal one-line goal, so the subagent is free to interpret it creatively","D. The complete conversation with the end user, so nothing about their intent is lost"],a:1,
e:"The guidance is that \"each subagent needs an objective, an output format, guidance on the tools and sources to use, and clear task boundaries.\" Each element prevents a specific observed failure: no objective produces work that misses the point; no output format produces returns the orchestrator cannot reconcile; no tool guidance produces expensive detours; and no boundaries produce **duplicated effort and gaps**, because \"without detailed task descriptions, agents duplicate work, leave gaps, or fail to find necessary information.\" Briefs are the main lever an orchestrator has over the quality and cost of a fan-out.",
w:"A — the full plan is mostly irrelevant context that consumes the subagent's window and invites it to stray outside its slice. C — an underspecified brief is the documented cause of duplicated work and coverage gaps; latitude is not the goal. D — the whole user conversation is bulk the subagent does not need; the orchestrator's job is to distil intent into a specific brief."},

{id:1751,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An orchestrator dispatches five research subagents with briefs like \"research the competitive landscape\". Three return substantially the same findings about the same two companies, and nothing at all is returned about pricing. What is the cause and the fix?",
o:["A. Too many subagents were spawned; three would have covered the same ground","B. The subagents lacked memory of each other's work; give them a shared scratchpad they all write to","C. The briefs had no boundaries, so coverage was neither partitioned nor complete; assign each subagent an explicit, disjoint slice with a stated output format","D. The model tier was too weak to research a broad topic; use a more capable model for each subagent"],a:2,
e:"**Overlap and gaps are the same defect**: unpartitioned briefs. Five agents given the same vague objective each follow the most obvious path, converge on the same prominent sources, and collectively leave the less obvious dimensions untouched. The orchestrator's decomposition must assign disjoint, jointly exhaustive slices — this subagent takes pricing, that one takes distribution — with the output format fixed so the returns compose. Verify coverage in the synthesis step against the intended slice list, so a missing dimension is detected rather than silently absent from the report.",
w:"A — three identically vague briefs produce three overlapping results; the count is not the problem, the specification is. B — a shared scratchpad adds contention and cross-talk and still does not decide who covers pricing; partitioning is the orchestrator's responsibility. D — a stronger model executes a vague brief more thoroughly, still overlaps with its peers, and still omits the dimension nobody was assigned."},

{id:1752,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An orchestrator spawns subagents on every request. Telemetry shows that for the 60% of requests that are simple single-fact lookups, it still spawns three to five subagents, and cost per request is roughly nine times the single-agent baseline. What is the appropriate remedy?",
o:["A. Cap the number of subagents at two for all requests, halving the worst-case cost","B. Move subagent spawning into a background queue so users do not wait for it","C. Instruct the orchestrator in its system prompt to be economical with subagents","D. Add a complexity assessment before fan-out: handle simple lookups directly with a bounded effort budget, and reserve fan-out for requests that meet explicit criteria, enforced in code"],a:3,
e:"Spawning excessive subagents for simple queries is a documented coordination failure, and it is where most of the multi-agent token multiplier is wasted. The remedy is a **scaling rule applied before the fan-out**: assess complexity, then allocate — a direct answer for a single-fact lookup, a small number of subagents for a comparison, a larger fan-out only for genuinely broad research — with the allocation and a hard agent and token cap enforced by the orchestration code rather than left to the model's discretion. Match effort to request, and make the ceiling a check, not an instruction.",
w:"A — a flat cap of two still pays fan-out overhead on every trivial lookup while starving the genuinely complex requests that needed more. B — backgrounding hides latency and changes nothing about cost; the wasted subagents still run. C — an instruction is unenforceable and unmeasured, and the model has no view of the budget, so the cap belongs in code."},

{id:1753,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How do latency and cost behave when an orchestrator fans out N independent subagents, compared with running the same N subtasks sequentially?",
o:["A. Latency approaches that of the slowest subagent rather than the sum, while token cost is roughly the same and coordination adds overhead","B. Both latency and token cost fall by roughly a factor of N","C. Latency is unchanged, because the model provider serialises requests from one account","D. Latency falls, and token cost falls because subagents share a cached prefix"],a:0,
e:"Parallel fan-out converts a **sum into a maximum** for wall-clock time: the orchestrator waits for the slowest worker, which is why parallelising subagents and their tool calls is reported to cut research time dramatically on complex queries. Tokens, however, are consumed by the same work regardless of ordering, and fan-out adds its own overhead — each subagent's system prompt and history, plus the orchestrator's briefs and synthesis. The honest summary: buy latency, do not expect to save tokens, and watch the straggler, because one slow worker sets the whole run's duration.",
w:"B — token cost does not divide by N; the work is the same and the coordination overhead is additive. C — concurrent requests are served concurrently, subject to rate limits, which throttle rather than serialise. D — a shared cached prefix can reduce the price of *identical* prefixes, but subagent briefs differ by design, and caching does not offset the added per-subagent overhead."},

{id:1754,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Subagents are asked in their prompts to \"return JSON with fields `claim`, `source` and `confidence`\". About 2% of returns are unparseable or omit a field, and the orchestrator's synthesis crashes or silently skips them. What is the strongest fix?",
o:["A. Add a retry that re-prompts the subagent whenever parsing fails, up to three attempts","B. Constrain the subagent's output with native structured output — `output_config.format` set to a `json_schema` — or a `strict` tool definition, so schema-valid output is guaranteed by constrained decoding, and treat any residual parse failure as a hard error","C. Ask the subagent to return Markdown tables instead, which are more robust to formatting drift","D. Prefill the assistant turn with an opening brace so the model must continue in JSON"],a:1,
e:"Asking for a schema is a request; **constraining decoding** is a guarantee. Setting `output_config.format` to `{\"type\":\"json_schema\",\"schema\":{...}}` — or marking the tool definition `strict: true` — makes the sampler unable to emit output that violates the schema, eliminating the missing-field and truncated-JSON classes at the source. Keep a hard failure path for anything that still does not parse, so returns are never silently skipped. **Currency note:** this native feature supersedes the old workaround of forcing a schema-bearing tool, and the `{`-prefill trick is not merely fragile — assistant prefill returns a **400** on current models.",
w:"A — retries pay for the same generation repeatedly and still leave a tail of failures; the invalid shape should be unrepresentable rather than re-attempted. C — Markdown tables are less machine-checkable than JSON and have no schema to validate against, so the same omissions recur undetected. D — assistant prefill is rejected with a `400` on current models, so this approach does not run at all."},

{id:1755,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pharmaceutical company's literature-review agent produces summaries that regulators may later examine. Which return-format requirement most directly serves that obligation?",
o:["A. Every summary must state the model identifier and the effort setting used","B. Every summary must be under 500 words so reviewers can read it quickly","C. Every claim must carry a machine-readable citation to the specific source and location it came from, persisted alongside the summary","D. Every summary must include the agent's self-reported confidence in its overall conclusion"],a:2,
e:"For an artefact that will be examined later, the load-bearing property is **traceability from claim to evidence**. A structured citation per claim — document identifier, version or date, section or page — persisted with the output lets any statement be checked against its source months later, and lets conflicts between sources be detected rather than smoothed away in prose. It also makes review efficient: a reviewer verifies the mapping instead of re-reading the corpus. Provenance is a schema requirement, not a stylistic one, so enforce it with structured output rather than requesting it in the prompt.",
w:"A — run metadata is worth logging but connects no particular claim to its evidence and cannot be used to verify a statement. B — brevity aids reading and works against evidence density; a short unsourced summary is the least auditable artefact of all. D — a self-reported overall confidence is uncalibrated and, being aggregate, points at no specific claim a reviewer could check."},

{id:1756,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A subagent needs to read records from a multi-tenant system. Its brief, written by the orchestrator from the user's request, includes `tenant_id: 4471`. What is the correct authorisation design?",
o:["A. Trust the `tenant_id` in the brief, since the orchestrator is a trusted internal component","B. Have the subagent verify the tenant by asking the orchestrator to confirm it before querying","C. Include the tenant ID in the subagent's system prompt so it cannot be overridden by later content","D. Scope the query in the data layer to the tenant of the authenticated session that initiated the run, ignoring any tenant identifier the model supplies"],a:3,
e:"Any value that passes through a model is **model-supplied input and therefore untrusted**, however trusted the component that emitted it. Retrieved content can contain injected instructions, and a paraphrasing chain can corrupt an identifier with no attack at all. Authorisation must be evaluated in code against the **authenticated session** that started the run — the tool implementation derives the tenant from the session and constrains the query, so a wrong or hostile `tenant_id` in the brief simply has no effect. Never authorise against an identity the model asserts.",
w:"A — the orchestrator is a model, not an authority; treating its output as an authorisation decision puts cross-tenant access one paraphrase or one injection away. B — asking another model to confirm a value adds a round trip and no security, since both sides are inside the untrusted zone. C — the system prompt is not a protected store; it constrains behaviour on average and enforces nothing, and the tenant is still asserted rather than derived."},

{id:1757,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent's payment-submission tool times out. The retry policy re-issues the call, and the customer is occasionally charged twice. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Make the write idempotent — the caller generates an idempotency key per logical action, and the service deduplicates retries of the same key","B. Remove retries from all write tools, and surface every timeout to the model to decide what to do","C. Extend the timeout so the call has time to complete before the client gives up","D. Have the agent query the payment history after each timeout and only retry if no charge is found"],a:0,
e:"A timeout is an **ambiguous outcome**: the request may have succeeded, failed, or still be in flight. Retrying an ambiguous non-idempotent write is therefore unsafe by construction, and no timeout value removes the ambiguity. The fix belongs at the interface: attach a caller-generated **idempotency key** to each logical payment so the service recognises and collapses a duplicate submission, making retries safe and letting the client retry freely. This is a property of the tool implementation, not of the agent's judgement — any write an agent can retry must be idempotent.",
w:"B — refusing to retry turns every transient blip into a failed payment, and asking the model to adjudicate an ambiguous timeout puts a guess in front of a financial action. C — a longer timeout narrows the window but leaves the same ambiguity whenever it does expire, and it degrades latency for every call. D — read-after-timeout races in-flight processing and depends on read consistency, reducing the frequency of double charges without eliminating them."},

{id:1758,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Logs from a fan-out agent system interleave entries from the orchestrator and eight concurrent subagents. Debugging a single user complaint means guessing which lines belong together. What is the minimum change that makes runs reconstructable?",
o:["A. Increase log verbosity so each entry contains the full prompt and response","B. Propagate a run-level trace identifier and a parent-child span identifier through every model call and tool call, and record the API `request-id` on each entry","C. Write each subagent's logs to a separate file and merge them manually when needed","D. Log only the orchestrator's activity, since it summarises what the subagents did"],a:1,
e:"Distributed agent runs need **correlation**, not volume. One identifier per user request, plus a span identifier recording which agent issued the call and which agent spawned it, lets you reconstruct the whole tree — order, nesting, duration, cost per branch — from interleaved output. Recording the API's `request-id` header on each entry ties your span to the provider-side record, which is what support escalations need. With that skeleton in place you can attach whatever payloads you want (prompts, tool arguments, termination reason, token usage) and still answer \"what happened on this run\" deterministically.",
w:"A — more text in uncorrelated lines makes the haystack larger; the missing information is which lines belong to which run and parent. C — per-file separation still loses ordering and the parent-child structure across files, and manual merging does not scale to eight concurrent branches. D — the orchestrator's own view omits the subagent tool calls and errors where the defect usually is, and its summary is a model output rather than a record."},

{id:1759,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"In an agent that plans a set of actions and then executes them, where should a human approval gate be placed?",
o:["A. Before planning, so the human authorises the goal and the agent then proceeds freely","B. After execution, so the human reviews what actually happened","C. Between planning and execution, immediately before the irreversible action, on the concrete action rather than on the intent","D. At every model call, so no step is unreviewed"],a:2,
e:"A gate is only useful where it can still **prevent** something, and it must attach to the artefact that will actually be executed. That places it after the plan exists and before the irreversible step, reviewing the concrete action — the diff, the payment, the deletion — rather than a description of an intention. Approving a goal up front authorises everything the agent might subsequently decide to do, and reviewing afterwards is monitoring, not oversight. Gate by impact and irreversibility so the routine majority runs unattended, and enforce the gate in code, not by asking the model to pause.",
w:"A — authorising the goal grants blanket consent to whatever plan follows, which is the widest possible approval and the least informed. B — post-hoc review cannot stop the action; it detects harm that has already occurred. D — gating every call is over-gating, producing approval fatigue in which reviewers rubber-stamp everything including the dangerous request."},

{id:1760,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team is tempted to convert a single agent into a multi-agent system for a task where each step must reason over the same shared design document, and every step depends on the conclusions of the one before. What outcome should they expect?",
o:["A. Better results, because each subagent can specialise in one aspect of the document","B. Roughly the same results at lower cost, because each subagent reads only part of the document","C. Better results, because separate context windows reduce the chance of the model losing track","D. Worse results at much higher cost: the shared context must be replicated to every subagent and the dependencies force serialisation, so coordination overhead dominates"],a:3,
e:"The published guidance names this as the anti-fit: multi-agent architectures are poor for \"domains that require all agents to share the same context, or involve many dependencies between agents.\" Both conditions hold. Replicating the design document into every subagent multiplies input tokens with no isolation benefit — the volume is shared, not partitionable — and a dependency chain removes the parallelism that would justify the fan-out, leaving serialised hand-offs that also lose intent at each boundary. A single agent with good context management is the correct design, and the multiplier (multi-agent runs around 15× a chat) is real money.",
w:"A — specialisation requires separable aspects; a chain of dependent conclusions over one document has none, and each specialist still needs the whole document. B — cost rises rather than falls, because the shared document is re-sent per subagent on top of the coordination overhead. C — separate windows help when each agent has *different* bulk material to absorb; with identical shared context they add cost and hand-off loss without reducing what anyone must read."},

{id:1761,d:"D3",s:"Agent Architecture",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Monthly spend on a multi-agent product has doubled and the team cannot tell which part is responsible. Which instrumentation answers the question?",
o:["A. Record token usage per model call, tagged with the run identifier, the agent role and the parent span, so spend rolls up by role and by run","B. Record the total tokens per run only, and compare the monthly averages","C. Take the provider's monthly invoice and divide it by the number of completed runs","D. Enable prompt caching everywhere, which will reduce the bill and make the cause moot"],a:0,
e:"Cost attribution needs the same dimensions as tracing. Reading `usage` from every response — input, output, and the cache creation and read counts — and tagging it with the **run, agent role and parent span** lets you aggregate spend by role (\"the pricing researcher is 60% of the bill\"), by run type and by depth, which turns a doubled invoice into a specific finding such as an over-eager fan-out or a subagent re-reading the same corpus. Aggregate-only figures tell you that something changed; per-role attribution tells you what to fix.",
w:"B — a per-run total shows the symptom and hides which agent or phase caused it, so it cannot direct any fix. C — an invoice-derived average blends every workload and product surface and cannot isolate a role or a run type. D — caching may or may not help depending on prefix stability, and enabling it without attribution risks paying `cache_creation_input_tokens` for prefixes that are never read again."}
);
